de hydrolysis across specified alkaline pH, temperature, and residence-time windows.\",\n        \"Compare alkaline and acidic processing experimentally, including aminonitrile back-reaction, degradation, and free-proline recovery.\",\n        \"Provide a physically justified pH-transition protocol and the same photochemical and sulfur-speciation documentation required for P1.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.87,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.83,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.90,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.75,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.64,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.84\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.78,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.84,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.57,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.77,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.74,\n      \"feedback\": \"The route to the cyclic aminonitrile retains the strengths of P1. Sulfide addition to a nitrile is plausible, but the specific proline-thioamide processing sequence is not established by the supplied studies. In particular, rxn_014 requires evidence that hydrolytic sulfur replacement gives prolinamide at useful rates and selectivity under the intended conditions. This is an experimental extension, not a demonstrated shortcut to environmental proline release. Its uncertainty concerns chemistry and conditions, not conservation.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Identify and quantify mol_019 during sulfide treatment of the actual proline aminonitrile.\",\n        \"Measure thioamide-to-amide conversion, competing products, and subsequent free-proline release under stated aqueous conditions.\",\n        \"Establish whether sulfur-assisted processing improves recovery relative to direct hydrolysis rather than assuming an advantage from reagent compatibility.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.75,\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.79,\n        \"rxn_017\": 0.53,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.87,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.83,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.90,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.82,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.84\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.70,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.80,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.55,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.72,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.69,\n      \"feedback\": \"The carbon-skeleton accounting and connection to the established cyanohydrin/thioamide sequence are coherent. Crossed aldol formation and dehydration are plausible, but their useful selectivity is not demonstrated here. The main entry bottleneck is rxn_017: ammonia can undergo competing carbonyl condensation, repeated addition, and oligomer-forming chemistry with acrolein, so selective production of recoverable 3-aminopropanal cannot be assumed. Explicitly labeling this entry as proposed is appropriate, but does not validate it. Acidic release of free proline remains a second unresolved process boundary.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Quantify crossed-aldol selectivity against acetaldehyde self-condensation and further hydroxymethylation.\",\n        \"Test acrolein ammoniation directly, identifying 3-aminopropanal and determining whether prompt cyanohydrin trapping can outcompete side reactions.\",\n        \"Specify compa