    \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7NOS\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-hydroxypyrrolidine-2-thione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7NS\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"pyrrolidine-2-thione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Gamma-butyrothiolactam\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Proline aminonitrile; stereounspecified inventory\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Prolinamide; stereounspecified inventory\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2S)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-Proline\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c7-5(8)4-2-1-3-6-4/h4,6H,1-3H2,(H,7,8)/t4-/m0/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2R)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"D-Proline\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c7-5(8)4-2-1-3-6-4/h4,6H,1-3H2,(H,7,8)/t4-/m1/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Methane\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dinitrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Molecular nitrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/N2/c1-2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Molecular hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Formic acid; proposed disproportionation coproduct\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O2/c2-1-3/h1H,(H,2,3)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-assisted acetylene hydrocyanation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)-cyanide complexes\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Candidate envelope: 320-350 K; not a reported step-specific temperature from the supplied summary\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar at a gas-water interface with local retention of volatile reactants\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)-cyanide complexes\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, sulfide-poor compartment supplied with HCN and acetylene. Candidate pH 7-9. Gas capture and reactant activities must be measured; dilute ocean water is not assumed sufficient. Keep sulfide out of this stage because copper sulfide precipitation can remove the catalyst.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Copper-mediated addition of HCN across acetylene gives acrylonitrile. Reference equation: mol_002 + mol_003 -> mol_007.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) connect HCN/acetylene chemistry to acrylonitrile and the proline branch. Copper is justified by this nitrile-generating chemistry, not by a generic preference for mineral catalysis. Natural acetylene supply, volatile capture and copper speciation remain integration bottlenecks. Rugheimer and Rimmer (2019) support possible atmospheric HCN supply, but do not establish the complete local feedstock assemblage or the candidate operating envelope.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia conjugate addition to acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Candidate envelope: 300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich water, candidate pH 9-10.5, with ammonia in excess over acrylonitrile. Use a shaded pore or a dark interval rather than unnecessary UV exposure. Candidate concentration screening: millimolar acrylonitrile with substantially higher total ammonia. Repeated addition and oligomerization must be quantified.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aza-Michael addition of ammonia at the beta carbon gives the C3 amino nitrile. Reference equation: mol_007 + mol_004 -> mol_008.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) report approximately 83% conversion in an optimized experimental segment. That result supports this transformation, not the numerical yield under the proposed environmental envelope. Excess ammonia favors primary addition relative to further alkylation, but naturally retaining ammonia while concentrating acrylonitrile remains unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-assisted sulfide photoreduction to 3-aminopropanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Photoactive copper-cyanide complexes\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Candidate envelope: 300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Copper-cyanide photoredox complexes\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shallow UV-exposed water; short-wave UV overlaps the laboratory photochemical regime. Candidate pH 7.5-9, millimolar organic substrate and reduced sulfur, with lower catalytic copper concentrations. Cyanide complexation may retain some dissolved copper, but Cu-sulfide precipitation must be measured. Capture the unstable aminoaldehyde promptly rather than storing it between stages.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sulfide supplies reducing equivalents for nitrile-to-imine reduction; imine hydrolysis releases ammonia and the aldehyde. Formal terminal-sulfur reference equation: 8 mol_008 + 8 mol_005 + 8 mol_001 -> 8 mol_009 + 8 mol_004 + mol_006. Actual oxidized sulfur may initially occupy polysulfide or other sulfur pools rather than pure S8.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) report approximately 45% conversion to beta-aminopropionaldehyde in their tested sequence. The substrate-product transformation is supported; the proposed field concentrations, solar residence time and exclusive S8 closure are not measured results. Sulfide is a consumed electron donor, not merely a catalyst. The sulfur equation is an explicitly idealized terminal redox balance and must not be used as an experimentally established sulfur flux.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional copper-omitted sulfide photoreduction\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Candidate envelope: 300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same substrate, reduced-sulfur supply, pH envelope and UV access as rxn_003, but no deliberately added copper at this photoreduction stage. Prior copper from rxn_001 must be separated or quantified before this can genuinely be tested as a copper-omitted branch. Photon dose, aldehyde yield and sulfur speciation are undetermined.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Direct sulfide photochemistry is proposed to supply reducing equivalents without a copper mediator. Formal reference equation: 8 mol_008 + 8 mol_005 + 8 mol_001 -> 8 mol_009 + 8 mol_004 + mol_006. The same unverified terminal-S8 convention applies as in rxn_003.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative substrate-specific extension. Patel et al. (2015) show that copper is not indispensable for every sulfide-driven photoreduction, but the supplied research does not establish copper omission specifically for this proline-branch substrate at useful yield. This branch tests a chemically motivated response to copper-sulfide sequestration. It is not counted as an independent experimental demonstration, and the overall pathway still uses copper upstream in rxn_001.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin homologation of 3-aminopropanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Candidate envelope: 300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Prompt HCN capture of freshly formed aminoaldehyde in a shaded compartment. Candidate pH 6.5-8.5 permits cyanide nucleophilicity while limiting prolonged alkaline aldehyde degradation. Residual ammonia can compete through imine/Strecker chemistry; dilution, partial ammonia loss and pH change require explicit mass-balance testing rather than assumed perfect separation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, followed by proton transfer, producing the C4 alpha-hydroxynitrile. Reference equation: mol_009 + mol_002 -> mol_010.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) report essentially quantitative cyanohydrin formation from the aminoaldehyde in the tested segment. This is a demonstrated carbon-skeleton extension. Its environmental success depends on rapid trapping and reagent sequencing; it does not establish that the preceding ammonia-rich stream can be transferred without loss or competing chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide addition to the cyanohydrin nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Candidate envelope: 300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Sulfide-bearing, anoxic water; candidate pH 7-9. Reduced sulfur is a stoichiometric sulfur source. A shaded or nighttime stage avoids assigning UV a role where it is unnecessary. Competing hydrolysis and reversible cyanohydrin dissociation must be measured.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrosulfide attacks the nitrile carbon; proton transfers give an alpha-hydroxythioamide. Neutral-reference equation: mol_010 + mol_005 -> mol_011.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) experimentally connect the aminoaldehyde cyanohydrin to an alpha-hydroxythioamide manifold. The acyclic and cyclic species belong to an interconverting reaction system, not necessarily separately accumulated environmental reservoirs. No unsupported clay or metal catalyst is added.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Five-membered thiolactam ring formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Candidate envelope: 300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Near-neutral to mildly alkaline, anoxic water. The terminal amino group must have an appreciable unprotonated fraction. This reaction can occur in the same local thioamide manifold as rxn_006; no artificial geographic transfer is required.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Intramolecular aminolysis of the thioamide closes a five-membered ring and expels ammonia. Reference equation: mol_011 -> mol_012 + mol_004.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide is part of the mechanistically resolved proline branch described by Patel et al. (2015). Five-membered intramolecular closure supplies the ring-forming logic missing from a generic ordinary Strecker assignment. The graph distinguishes this structural event without asserting isolation or accumulation of every member of the experimental thioamide manifold.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Photoreductive deoxygenation to gamma-butyrothiolactam\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Candidate envelope: 300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Renewed UV exposure in an anoxic sulfide-bearing shallow compartment, after formation of the cyclic hydroxythioamide. Candidate pH 7-9. Reduced sulfur supplies reducing equivalents. Copper is not imposed as a universally required catalyst; any carryover must be analytically recorded.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net reductive replacement of the ring hydroxyl group by hydrogen retains the thiolactam. Formal terminal-sulfur reference equation: 8 mol_012 + 8 mol_005 -> 8 mol_013 + 8 mol_001 + mol_006.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) describe irradiation/reduction giving gamma-butyrothiolactam. The organic transformation is supported, whereas the chosen terminal-S8 balance and candidate field envelope are modeling assumptions. Actual sulfur products and the importance of residual copper require measurement. Continuous irradiation of the entire upstream mixture is not assumed to reproduce the staged laboratory chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Thiolactam photoreduction followed by cyanide trapping\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Candidate envelope: 300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Stage photoreduction in an anoxic reduced-sulfur compartment, then expose the reactive reduced intermediate to HCN before it is lost to hydrolysis or polymerization. Candidate pH 7-9. This is an ordered two-operation module, not a claim that simultaneous addition of every network reagent is productive.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reduction and sulfur departure generate a transient cyclic imine/iminium-equivalent that is trapped by cyanide. No independently isolated imine node is asserted. Formal terminal-sulfur equation: 8 mol_013 + 8 mol_005 + 8 mol_002 -> 8 mol_014 + 8 mol_005 + mol_006. In this idealized redox bookkeeping, donor sulfide is oxidized while thiolactam-bound sulfur is released as sulfide; the common H2S inventory therefore occurs on both sides and cancels in the net pool balance.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) report the final two-step conversion to the proline aminonitrile at approximately 32% in the tested segment. This is the demonstrated endpoint, not a measured free-proline synthesis. The transient-imine interpretation is mechanistic rather than evidence of an isolated reservoir. The detailed sulfur-product assignment remains provisional; the equation records a possible balanced terminal projection, not measured sulfur atom provenance or a claim that no reducing equivalents are required.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Proposed acidic surface hydration of proline aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium supplied by a volcanic acid reservoir\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Candidate envelope: 350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"H+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Candidate pH 1-2 in HCl-bearing geothermal water; molecular oxygen is unnecessary. Transfer the completed aminonitrile inventory out of the photochemical compartment before acidification. Screen residence times from days to weeks rather than assume a known environmental rate. Acidification can volatilize residual HCN and H2S, so their loss and the associated material balance must be included in any implementation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-assisted nitrile hydration gives the carboxamide. Neutral-reference equation: mol_014 + mol_001 -> mol_015. Actual reactant and product protonation states are governed by the acid reservoir.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Proposed environmental extension, not demonstrated in the supplied proline experiments. Patel et al. (2015) establish the aminonitrile precursor; general nitrile chemistry motivates hydration through an amide. The supplied research does not establish rate, yield or selectivity at this surface pH-temperature combination. Amide formation, precursor loss and competing decomposition are explicit bottlenecks.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Proposed acidic surface hydrolysis of prolinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium supplied by a volcanic acid reservoir\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Candidate envelope: 350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"H+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Continue in the acidic compartment used for rxn_010, candidate pH 1-2, while measuring amide disappearance and free amino-acid production. Cool after productive conversion. Protonated proline and ammonium are the actual acidic solution forms; the graph uses neutral molecular references. No crystallization-based or mineral-based L selection is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed acyl substitution hydrolyzes the carboxamide without requiring cleavage of the pyrrolidine ring. Racemic-inventory reference equation: 2 mol_015 + 2 mol_001 -> mol_016 + mol_017 + 2 mol_004.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Proposed extension beyond Patel et al. (2015), whose demonstrated endpoint is the aminonitrile. Conventional amide hydrolysis makes this chemically justified, but useful rates and preservation of the product under the candidate conditions have not been established by the supplied studies. Equal L and D coefficients express the achiral model assumption, not a measured enantiomeric analysis or an L-selective process.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Proposed alkaline surface hydration of proline aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide maintained by alkaline rock-water interaction\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Candidate envelope: 350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"OH\u2212\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Candidate pH 10-11 in a warm alkaline pool supplied by rock-water interaction. Separate this processing stage temporally from aldehyde chemistry. Screen days-to-months residence times while measuring degradation; these are experimental screening windows, not predicted reaction times. Water remains present during hydrolysis rather than assuming that complete drying promotes it.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide-assisted attack on the nitrile followed by proton transfers gives prolinamide. Neutral-reference equation: mol_014 + mol_001 -> mol_015.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Proposed environmental extension. Patel et al. (2015) support the precursor but not alkaline release of free proline. Base-assisted nitrile hydration is chemically conventional, yet sufficiently rapid conversion at pH 10-11 cannot be assumed. This branch tests compatibility with naturally alkaline water and is not ranked above the acidic alternative without kinetic data.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Proposed alkaline surface hydrolysis of prolinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Alkaline aqueous hydroxide reservoir\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Candidate envelope: 350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"OH\u2212-promoted hydrolysis; alkalinity is consumed when carboxylate is retained\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Candidate pH 10-11, maintained by continued alkaline inflow or buffering rather than assumed catalytic turnover of a finite amount of base. Product is predominantly prolinate under alkaline conditions. Cooling and subsequent pH equilibration change speciation, not the molecular carbon skeleton. Monitor hydrolysis, decomposition and racemization separately.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Base-promoted amide hydrolysis yields prolinate and ammonia; protonation equilibria are outside the molecular reference equation. Neutral-reference equation: 2 mol_015 + 2 mol_001 -> mol_016 + mol_017 + 2 mol_004.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Proposed extension beyond the aminonitrile endpoint of Patel et al. (2015). The distinction between hydroxide promotion and true catalytic base turnover matters because alkaline amide hydrolysis retains product as carboxylate. Neither the rate under the proposed conditions nor an advantage over acidic processing is established by the supplied literature. This route produces an expected DL inventory, not enantioenriched L-proline.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Proposed moderately hydrothermal aminonitrile hydration\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Acidic hydrothermal proton reservoir\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Candidate envelope: 393-423 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-30 bar, maintaining liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"H+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Candidate pH 2-4 in a cooler acidic hydrothermal margin, not the hottest vent endmember. This branch requires physical infiltration or delivery of the surface-made aminonitrile into a hydrothermal circulation path; that transfer is proposed, not demonstrated. Screen residence times from hours to one day and measure precursor survival. No UV is required at depth.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Thermally accelerated acid-assisted nitrile hydration gives the same prolinamide intermediate. Reference equation: mol_014 + mol_001 -> mol_015.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative environmental implementation of conventional nitrile hydration. Patel et al. (2015) supply the defined precursor but do not demonstrate this transfer or hydrolysis regime. Hennet et al. (1992) establish that amino-acid chemistry can operate near 423 K, not that this particular nitrile hydrolyzes selectively there. Higher temperature may improve hydration while increasing destructive side reactions; both fluxes require measurement.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Proposed hydrothermal prolinamide hydrolysis with rapid quenching\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Acidic hydrothermal proton reservoir\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Candidate reaction envelope: 393-423 K; quench outlet to below 320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-30 bar during the hot aqueous stage\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"H+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Candidate pH 2-4. Use a flowing moderate-temperature zone, measure the time of maximum free-proline recovery, and mix the outlet promptly with cooler anoxic water. Hours-to-one-day residence times are a screening envelope only. Independently supplied H2-rich surroundings may reduce some degradation but are not assumed to make proline indefinitely stable.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of the amide side group releases ammonia and the amino-acid carboxyl functionality. Reference equation: 2 mol_015 + 2 mol_001 -> mol_016 + mol_017 + 2 mol_004. Quenching is a physical processing requirement, not an additional synthetic reaction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Proposed extension beyond Patel et al. (2015). Bada, Miller and Zhao (1995) demonstrate the importance of irreversible amino-acid destruction at substantially higher temperatures; Yanagawa and Kobayashi (1997) show that hydrogen fugacity can alter decomposition behavior. These studies justify testing short residence times and reducing conditions, not assuming a measured proline yield or a validated hydrothermal advantage.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Empirical reducing-atmosphere discharge and aqueous recovery module\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_019\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 300-370 K in the collection water; discharge channels are transient nonthermal high-energy regions\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"CH4/N2/H2O with trace ammonia and electrical discharge, followed by collection in water. Assign the setting to a transient impact-reduced or locally reduced atmosphere, not a persistent global methane atmosphere. The supplied report does not specify all original run durations or workup parameters. Record silicate vessel or shoreline contact explicitly, because the surface contribution is not established to be zero.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Unresolved radical, carbonyl, nitrogen-fixation and ring-construction network, lumped with aqueous recovery. No ordinary-Strecker-only mechanism is asserted. A balanced target-channel reference is: 20 mol_018 + mol_019 + 2 mol_004 + 8 mol_001 -> 2 mol_016 + 2 mol_017 + 33 mol_020. This is not the measured bulk gas balance, nitrogen-source partition, yield or coproduct inventory.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ring et al. (1972) detected proline, reported at approximately 1.5 micromol, in a complex discharge product mixture. Criado-Reyes et al. (2021) show that silicate-containing reactor surfaces can substantially change discharge chemistry, without establishing a specific proline catalyst here. Trail, Watson and Tailby (2011) challenge a persistently strongly reducing atmosphere. The empirical endpoint is supported; the reference equation is a formally balanced projection, and L:D equality is an achiral expectation rather than an enantiomer-resolved result.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Empirical hot formaldehyde-ammonia synthesis and hydrolytic recovery\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_023\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Acid used only in the separate analytical hydrolysis workup\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Reported synthesis temperature approximately 458 K; cool before separate hydrolytic workup\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Candidate aqueous-retention envelope: 15-25 bar at 458 K; original pressure is not specified in the supplied summary\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"None for the thermal synthesis; H+ during analytical hydrolysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated formaldehyde/ammonia in a closed hot aqueous system. The supplied report does not provide enough information to assign exact feed concentrations, residence time or original hydrolysis conditions. A separate strong-acid laboratory hydrolysis benchmark must therefore be documented explicitly rather than silently modeled as a natural pond process. Cool and dilute outflow before prolonged amino-acid exposure to heat.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Empirical condensation/disproportionation chemistry builds unidentified hydrolysable material that releases proline during workup. No molecular identity is assigned to the unresolved bulk precursor. A balanced reference projection is: 12 mol_021 + 2 mol_004 -> mol_016 + mol_017 + 2 mol_023 + 4 mol_001. Formic acid is a proposed formaldehyde-oxidation coproduct, not an experimentally established stoichiometric product of this proline branch.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Fox and Windsor (1970) report proline after hydrolysis of heated formaldehyde/ammonia products. Their experiment supports laboratory recovery, not a mapped C5-ring mechanism or autonomous environmental free-proline accumulation. Wolman et al. (1971) and the historical acid-blank concerns motivate rigorous blanks and isotope tracing. The formate closure is explicitly speculative: it supplies a chemically plausible disproportionation balance but cannot establish actual reaction flux or product distribution.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Empirical lower-molybdate formaldehyde-hydroxylamine and hydrolysis module\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_023\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Molybdate, 1e-4 M nominal concentration\",\n        \"Kaolinite, approximately 2% w/v\",\n        \"Montmorillonite, approximately 2% w/v\",\n        \"Zn2+, Fe3+, Cu2+, Co2+ and Mn2+, each nominally 1e-4 M in the experimental medium\",\n        \"Acid used in the separate analytical hydrolysis workup\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Reported synthesis: 378 \u00b1 5 K for seven days\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Sealed under N2; candidate geologic analogue 5-10 bar; original total pressure is not supplied\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Empirical multicomponent metal/clay selectivity medium; no unique active catalyst established\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reported starting conditions: 0.30 M formaldehyde, 0.05 M hydroxylamine and initial pH 5.5, followed by strong-acid analytical hydrolysis. The 1e-4 M molybdate condition is used rather than the much more problematic 1e-2 M maximum-selectivity condition. A confined hydrothermal pore is the proposed temperature-pressure analogue; an open ambient-pressure pool at 378 K is not assumed. Fe3+ denotes the nominal laboratory addition, not a persistent ferric ocean reservoir; its rapid reaction in a reducing, Fe2+-rich setting must be tested.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Unresolved oxime, condensation and possible cyanide chemistry generates hydrolysable proline-bearing material. The empirical metal/clay medium changes selectivity, but the specific ring-forming mechanism is unknown. Balanced reference projection: 14 mol_021 + 2 mol_022 -> mol_016 + mol_017 + 4 mol_023 + 4 mol_001. Formate is a proposed redox-balance coproduct, not a measured branch-specific material balance.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hatanaka and Egami (1977, Journal of Biochemistry; 1977, Bulletin of the Chemical Society of Japan) support this experimental family; the reported proline fraction at 1e-4 M molybdate is about 0.16 mol% of analyzed residues, not the 2.75 mol% associated with 1e-2 M. Scott et al. (2008) make even the lower molybdate inventory a geochemical enrichment problem. Hydroxylamine production, reagent concentration, persistence of the nominal mixed-metal medium, and natural replacement of strong-acid workup remain unresolved. This is a conditional laboratory-supported branch, not a demonstrated closed Hadean synthesis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Empirical reducing hydrothermal cyanide-carbonyl-ammonium module\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_021\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite assemblage as an experimental redox buffer and mineral interface\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Reported synthesis approximately 423 K; proposed outlet quench below 320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Reported approximately 10 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite redox-buffering assemblage; a specific proline-forming catalytic site is not established\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"HCN/KCN, formaldehyde and NH4Cl in reducing hot water. NH3 is the neutral reference node for total ammoniacal nitrogen; actual NH3/NH4+ speciation depends on in situ pH and temperature. The experiments used highly concentrated organics, described in the supplied report as tenths-of-molar-scale rather than natural vent-fluid measurements. Exact run-specific concentrations, pH, H2 fugacity and residence time must be taken from the original protocol, not invented. The proposed natural implementation uses independently supplied serpentinization H2, a moderate-temperature reaction zone and prompt cold-water mixing; it does not require transfer of discharge-produced H2.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Empirical hot cyanide/carbonyl/ammonium network with unresolved proline ring construction. A balanced target-channel projection is: mol_002 + 9 mol_021 + mol_004 + 3 mol_020 -> mol_016 + mol_017 + 5 mol_001. This equation is neither the measured bulk material balance nor a demonstrated elementary Strecker sequence, and it does not establish nitrogen atom provenance.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hennet, Holm and Engel (1992) detected proline under simulated hydrothermal conditions and explicitly did not claim that their precursor concentrations represented natural fluids. Bada, Miller and Zhao (1995) require inclusion of thermal destruction; Yanagawa and Kobayashi (1997) motivate examining hydrogen fugacity rather than assuming identical degradation in all redox regimes. The mineral assemblage is justified as the experimental environmental buffer, not as an invented selective cyclization catalyst. Feedstock capture, actual residence-time-dependent yield and survival during mixing remain unquantified.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-assisted cyanosulfidic scaffold with acidic surface release\",\n      \"description\": \"The best-resolved scaffold sequence reaches the experimentally demonstrated proline aminonitrile, then uses proposed acidic hydration and amide hydrolysis in a separate warm surface acid reservoir. Product is an expected DL inventory containing L-proline. The final hydrolysis sequence, sulfur speciation and autonomous reagent ordering remain unvalidated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-assisted cyanosulfidic scaffold with alkaline surface release\",\n      \"description\": \"Shares the demonstrated scaffold chemistry with P1 but processes the final aminonitrile in an alkaline warm-water reservoir. This is a process alternative, not an independent scaffold demonstration. Hydrolysis at environmentally accessible alkalinity may be slow, and no acid-versus-base yield advantage is asserted.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-assisted surface scaffold with moderately hydrothermal release\",\n      \"description\": \"Transfers the completed surface aminonitrile inventory into a moderately hot acidic aqueous circulation zone for proposed hydration and amide hydrolysis, followed by quenching. The required surface-to-subsurface transfer is an explicit integration gap. Faster hydrolysis cannot be assumed to outweigh thermal losses without kinetic measurements.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_015\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-omitted aminoaldehyde photoreduction with acidic surface release\",\n      \"description\": \"Replaces only the aminopropionitrile photoreduction with the explicitly speculative copper-omitted variant, then follows the shared ring-construction sequence and acidic surface hydrolysis. Copper remains involved in upstream acrylonitrile formation. This path tests catalyst dependence and is not an independently demonstrated synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-omitted aminoaldehyde photoreduction with alkaline surface release\",\n      \"description\": \"Combines the substrate-specific copper-omission hypothesis with alkaline hydration and amide hydrolysis. Both the copper-free conversion efficiency at rxn_004 and the final alkaline release rate require demonstration. The downstream ring chemistry does not validate either extension.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-omitted aminoaldehyde photoreduction with hydrothermal release\",\n      \"description\": \"Combines the proposed copper-omitted photoreduction stage with the surface scaffold sequence and a proposed moderate-temperature hydrothermal hydrolysis outlet. It shares the chemical evidence of P1-P5 and adds no independent experimental validation. Copper carryover, transport, hydrolysis and degradation are separate bottlenecks.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_015\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Transient reducing-atmosphere electrical-discharge route\",\n      \"description\": \"Empirically supported access to proline from simple gases and water, represented without inventing a ring-forming intermediate. The low-abundance laboratory endpoint does not establish a natural production flux, a global reducing atmosphere, a complete coproduct balance or L-selectivity.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Thermal formaldehyde-ammonia hydrolysate route\",\n      \"description\": \"Hot aqueous formaldehyde/ammonia chemistry yields material from which proline is recovered after hydrolysis. This empirical synthesis-and-workup module avoids naming an uncharacterized precursor as a molecule. Its major gaps are natural concentration, precursor identity, redox coproducts and replacement of analytical hydrolysis by an environmental release process.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Lower-molybdate formaldehyde-hydroxylamine hydrolysate route\",\n      \"description\": \"Uses the reported 1e-4 M molybdate member of the multicomponent metal/clay experiment rather than importing the 10 mM maximum-selectivity result. Proline recovery is experimentally supported at the selected condition, but hydroxylamine supply, local molybdate enrichment, ferric-metal persistence and natural hydrolysis remain unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_018\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Reducing hydrothermal cyanide-carbonyl-ammonium route\",\n      \"description\": \"Empirically supported hydrothermal access to proline at approximately 423 K and 10 bar with a justified Fe-S/oxide redox assemblage. The ring-forming mechanism and natural precursor activities are unresolved. Independently available hydrothermal H2 and rapid quenching are proposed environmental requirements, not demonstrated closure of the natural synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_015\",\n    \"mol_016\",\n    \"mol_017\",\n    \"mol_021\",\n    \"mol_023\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\",\n        \"rxn_018\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\",\n        \"rxn_018\",\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Proline", "overall_strategy": "Construct the proline skeleton through five literature-supported route families: the structurally resolved cyanosulfidic sequence, electrical-discharge chemistry, thermal formaldehyde/ammonia chemistry, formaldehyde/hydroxylamine chemistry, and hydrothermal cyanide/carbonyl/ammonium chemistry. The ten listed pathways are distinct process-level reaction sequences, not ten independently demonstrated scaffold syntheses: P1-P6 combine one cyanosulfidic scaffold sequence with alternative photoreduction and hydrolysis conditions; P7-P10 are empirical synthesis-and-recovery modules. No surveyed study establishes an autonomous, geochemically integrated synthesis of predominantly L-proline. Accordingly, L-proline and D-proline are explicit coproducts, with an expected racemic distribution rather than an asserted measured enantiomeric ratio. No unsupported resolution step is included. Patel et al. (2015) establish the cyclic aminonitrile endpoint; its environmental conversion to free proline remains a proposed extension. Numerical operating envelopes identified as candidate conditions are testable proposals, not reported experimental protocols or measured environmental concentrations. Historical endpoint experiments are represented as explicitly lumped modules because their ring-forming intermediates are unresolved; analytical acid hydrolysis is not equated with natural monomer availability. Reference equations in mechanism fields ensure elemental balance and an explicitly symmetric L:D representation, but empirical-module coefficients and sulfur/formate coproduct assignments are not measured process balances. Molecular formulas use neutral reference forms; aqueous protonation equilibria are implicit. Stereounspecified chiral intermediates represent unresolved enantiomeric inventories. The restricted role vocabulary requires terminal coproducts to be labeled intermediate; mol_006, mol_017 and mol_023 are coproduct reservoirs, not obligatory downstream intermediates. A null environment_formed denotes an externally supplied species or a species formed in multiple environments. Extraterrestrial ice and irradiated-formamide routes are excluded because their demonstrated conditions do not fit the permitted terrestrial environments. The especially problematic 10 mM molybdate condition is also excluded.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "water", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "C2H2", "iupac_name": "ethyne", "common_name": "Acetylene", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide; neutral reference for aqueous H2S/HS\u2212", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "S8", "iupac_name": "cyclooctasulfur", "common_name": "Elemental sulfur; terminal sulfur-balance reference", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C3H3N", "iupac_name": "prop-2-enenitrile", "common_name": "Acrylonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H3N/c1-2-3-4/h2H,1H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "3-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "Beta-aminopropionitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C3H7NO", "iupac_name": "3-aminopropanal", "common_name": "Beta-aminopropionaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C4H8N2O", "iupac_name": "4-amino-2-hydroxybutanenitrile", "common_name": "3-Aminopropanal cyanohydrin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C4H10N2OS", "iupac_name": "4-amino-2-hydroxybutanethioamide", "common_name": "Acyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C4H7NOS", "iupac_name": "3-hydroxypyrrolidine-2-thione", "common_name": "Cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C4H7NS", "iupac_name": "pyrrolidine-2-thione", "common_name": "Gamma-butyrothiolactam", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C5H8N2", "iupac_name": "pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile", "common_name": "Proline aminonitrile; stereounspecified inventory", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H10N2O", "iupac_name": "pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide", "common_name": "Prolinamide; stereounspecified inventory", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C5H9NO2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "L-Proline", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c7-5(8)4-2-1-3-6-4/h4,6H,1-3H2,(H,7,8)/t4-/m0/s1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C5H9NO2", "iupac_name": "(2R)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "D-Proline", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c7-5(8)4-2-1-3-6-4/h4,6H,1-3H2,(H,7,8)/t4-/m1/s1", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "methane", "common_name": "Methane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "N2", "iupac_name": "dinitrogen", "common_name": "Molecular nitrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/N2/c1-2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "Molecular hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "Formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "H3NO", "iupac_name": "hydroxylamine", "common_name": "Hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "CH2O2", "iupac_name": "methanoic acid", "common_name": "Formic acid; proposed disproportionation coproduct", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O2/c2-1-3/h1H,(H,2,3)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Copper-assisted acetylene hydrocyanation", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)-cyanide complexes"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Candidate envelope: 320-350 K; not a reported step-specific temperature from the supplied summary", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar at a gas-water interface with local retention of volatile reactants", "catalyst": "Cu(I)-cyanide complexes", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, sulfide-poor compartment supplied with HCN and acetylene. Candidate pH 7-9. Gas capture and reactant activities must be measured; dilute ocean water is not assumed sufficient. Keep sulfide out of this stage because copper sulfide precipitation can remove the catalyst."}, "mechanism": "Copper-mediated addition of HCN across acetylene gives acrylonitrile. Reference equation: mol_002 + mol_003 -> mol_007.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) connect HCN/acetylene chemistry to acrylonitrile and the proline branch. Copper is justified by this nitrile-generating chemistry, not by a generic preference for mineral catalysis. Natural acetylene supply, volatile capture and copper speciation remain integration bottlenecks. Rugheimer and Rimmer (2019) support possible atmospheric HCN supply, but do not establish the complete local feedstock assemblage or the candidate operating envelope."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Ammonia conjugate addition to acrylonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Candidate envelope: 300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia-rich water, candidate pH 9-10.5, with ammonia in excess over acrylonitrile. Use a shaded pore or a dark interval rather than unnecessary UV exposure. Candidate concentration screening: millimolar acrylonitrile with substantially higher total ammonia. Repeated addition and oligomerization must be quantified."}, "mechanism": "Aza-Michael addition of ammonia at the beta carbon gives the C3 amino nitrile. Reference equation: mol_007 + mol_004 -> mol_008.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) report approximately 83% conversion in an optimized experimental segment. That result supports this transformation, not the numerical yield under the proposed environmental envelope. Excess ammonia favors primary addition relative to further alkylation, but naturally retaining ammonia while concentrating acrylonitrile remains unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Copper-assisted sulfide photoreduction to 3-aminopropanal", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_005", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Photoactive copper-cyanide complexes"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Candidate envelope: 300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Copper-cyanide photoredox complexes", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shallow UV-exposed water; short-wave UV overlaps the laboratory photochemical regime. Candidate pH 7.5-9, millimolar organic substrate and reduced sulfur, with lower catalytic copper concentrations. Cyanide complexation may retain some dissolved copper, but Cu-sulfide precipitation must be measured. Capture the unstable aminoaldehyde promptly rather than storing it between stages."}, "mechanism": "Sulfide supplies reducing equivalents for nitrile-to-imine reduction; imine hydrolysis releases ammonia and the aldehyde. Formal terminal-sulfur reference equation: 8 mol_008 + 8 mol_005 + 8 mol_001 -> 8 mol_009 + 8 mol_004 + mol_006. Actual oxidized sulfur may initially occupy polysulfide or other sulfur pools rather than pure S8.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) report approximately 45% conversion to beta-aminopropionaldehyde in their tested sequence. The substrate-product transformation is supported; the proposed field concentrations, solar residence time and exclusive S8 closure are not measured results. Sulfide is a consumed electron donor, not merely a catalyst. The sulfur equation is an explicitly idealized terminal redox balance and must not be used as an experimentally established sulfur flux."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Conditional copper-omitted sulfide photoreduction", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_005", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Candidate envelope: 300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Same substrate, reduced-sulfur supply, pH envelope and UV access as rxn_003, but no deliberately added copper at this photoreduction stage. Prior copper from rxn_001 must be separated or quantified before this can genuinely be tested as a copper-omitted branch. Photon dose, aldehyde yield and sulfur speciation are undetermined."}, "mechanism": "Direct sulfide photochemistry is proposed to supply reducing equivalents without a copper mediator. Formal reference equation: 8 mol_008 + 8 mol_005 + 8 mol_001 -> 8 mol_009 + 8 mol_004 + mol_006. The same unverified terminal-S8 convention applies as in rxn_003.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific extension. Patel et al. (2015) show that copper is not indispensable for every sulfide-driven photoreduction, but the supplied research does not establish copper omission specifically for this proline-branch substrate at useful yield. This branch tests a chemically motivated response to copper-sulfide sequestration. It is not counted as an independent experimental demonstration, and the overall pathway still uses copper upstream in rxn_001."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Cyanohydrin homologation of 3-aminopropanal", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Candidate envelope: 300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Prompt HCN capture of freshly formed aminoaldehyde in a shaded compartment. Candidate pH 6.5-8.5 permits cyanide nucleophilicity while limiting prolonged alkaline aldehyde degradation. Residual ammonia can compete through imine/Strecker chemistry; dilution, partial ammonia loss and pH change require explicit mass-balance testing rather than assumed perfect separation."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, followed by proton transfer, producing the C4 alpha-hydroxynitrile. Reference equation: mol_009 + mol_002 -> mol_010.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) report essentially quantitative cyanohydrin formation from the aminoaldehyde in the tested segment. This is a demonstrated carbon-skeleton extension. Its environmental success depends on rapid trapping and reagent sequencing; it does not establish that the preceding ammonia-rich stream can be transferred without loss or competing chemistry."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Sulfide addition to the cyanohydrin nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Candidate envelope: 300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Sulfide-bearing, anoxic water; candidate pH 7-9. Reduced sulfur is a stoichiometric sulfur source. A shaded or nighttime stage avoids assigning UV a role where it is unnecessary. Competing hydrolysis and reversible cyanohydrin dissociation must be measured."}, "mechanism": "Hydrosulfide attacks the nitrile carbon; proton transfers give an alpha-hydroxythioamide. Neutral-reference equation: mol_010 + mol_005 -> mol_011.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) experimentally connect the aminoaldehyde cyanohydrin to an alpha-hydroxythioamide manifold. The acyclic and cyclic species belong to an interconverting reaction system, not necessarily separately accumulated environmental reservoirs. No unsupported clay or metal catalyst is added."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Five-membered thiolactam ring formation", "inputs": ["mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Candidate envelope: 300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Near-neutral to mildly alkaline, anoxic water. The terminal amino group must have an appreciable unprotonated fraction. This reaction can occur in the same local thioamide manifold as rxn_006; no artificial geographic transfer is required."}, "mechanism": "Intramolecular aminolysis of the thioamide closes a five-membered ring and expels ammonia. Reference equation: mol_011 -> mol_012 + mol_004.", "reasoning": "The cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide is part of the mechanistically resolved proline branch described by Patel et al. (2015). Five-membered intramolecular closure supplies the ring-forming logic missing from a generic ordinary Strecker assignment. The graph distinguishes this structural event without asserting isolation or accumulation of every member of the experimental thioamide manifold."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Photoreductive deoxygenation to gamma-butyrothiolactam", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Candidate envelope: 300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Renewed UV exposure in an anoxic sulfide-bearing shallow compartment, after formation of the cyclic hydroxythioamide. Candidate pH 7-9. Reduced sulfur supplies reducing equivalents. Copper is not imposed as a universally required catalyst; any carryover must be analytically recorded."}, "mechanism": "Net reductive replacement of the ring hydroxyl group by hydrogen retains the thiolactam. Formal terminal-sulfur reference equation: 8 mol_012 + 8 mol_005 -> 8 mol_013 + 8 mol_001 + mol_006.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) describe irradiation/reduction giving gamma-butyrothiolactam. The organic transformation is supported, whereas the chosen terminal-S8 balance and candidate field envelope are modeling assumptions. Actual sulfur products and the importance of residual copper require measurement. Continuous irradiation of the entire upstream mixture is not assumed to reproduce the staged laboratory chemistry."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Thiolactam photoreduction followed by cyanide trapping", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_005", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Candidate envelope: 300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Stage photoreduction in an anoxic reduced-sulfur compartment, then expose the reactive reduced intermediate to HCN before it is lost to hydrolysis or polymerization. Candidate pH 7-9. This is an ordered two-operation module, not a claim that simultaneous addition of every network reagent is productive."}, "mechanism": "Reduction and sulfur departure generate a transient cyclic imine/iminium-equivalent that is trapped by cyanide. No independently isolated imine node is asserted. Formal terminal-sulfur equation: 8 mol_013 + 8 mol_005 + 8 mol_002 -> 8 mol_014 + 8 mol_005 + mol_006. In this idealized redox bookkeeping, donor sulfide is oxidized while thiolactam-bound sulfur is released as sulfide; the common H2S inventory therefore occurs on both sides and cancels in the net pool balance.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) report the final two-step conversion to the proline aminonitrile at approximately 32% in the tested segment. This is the demonstrated endpoint, not a measured free-proline synthesis. The transient-imine interpretation is mechanistic rather than evidence of an isolated reservoir. The detailed sulfur-product assignment remains provisional; the equation records a possible balanced terminal projection, not measured sulfur atom provenance or a claim that no reducing equivalents are required."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Proposed acidic surface hydration of proline aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium supplied by a volcanic acid reservoir"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Candidate envelope: 350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "H+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Candidate pH 1-2 in HCl-bearing geothermal water; molecular oxygen is unnecessary. Transfer the completed aminonitrile inventory out of the photochemical compartment before acidification. Screen residence times from days to weeks rather than assume a known environmental rate. Acidification can volatilize residual HCN and H2S, so their loss and the associated material balance must be included in any implementation."}, "mechanism": "Acid-assisted nitrile hydration gives the carboxamide. Neutral-reference equation: mol_014 + mol_001 -> mol_015. Actual reactant and product protonation states are governed by the acid reservoir.", "reasoning": "Proposed environmental extension, not demonstrated in the supplied proline experiments. Patel et al. (2015) establish the aminonitrile precursor; general nitrile chemistry motivates hydration through an amide. The supplied research does not establish rate, yield or selectivity at this surface pH-temperature combination. Amide formation, precursor loss and competing decomposition are explicit bottlenecks."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Proposed acidic surface hydrolysis of prolinamide", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium supplied by a volcanic acid reservoir"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Candidate envelope: 350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "H+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Continue in the acidic compartment used for rxn_010, candidate pH 1-2, while measuring amide disappearance and free amino-acid production. Cool after productive conversion. Protonated proline and ammonium are the actual acidic solution forms; the graph uses neutral molecular references. No crystallization-based or mineral-based L selection is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed acyl substitution hydrolyzes the carboxamide without requiring cleavage of the pyrrolidine ring. Racemic-inventory reference equation: 2 mol_015 + 2 mol_001 -> mol_016 + mol_017 + 2 mol_004.", "reasoning": "Proposed extension beyond Patel et al. (2015), whose demonstrated endpoint is the aminonitrile. Conventional amide hydrolysis makes this chemically justified, but useful rates and preservation of the product under the candidate conditions have not been established by the supplied studies. Equal L and D coefficients express the achiral model assumption, not a measured enantiomeric analysis or an L-selective process."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Proposed alkaline surface hydration of proline aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide maintained by alkaline rock-water interaction"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Candidate envelope: 350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "OH\u2212", "aqueous": true, "other": "Candidate pH 10-11 in a warm alkaline pool supplied by rock-water interaction. Separate this processing stage temporally from aldehyde chemistry. Screen days-to-months residence times while measuring degradation; these are experimental screening windows, not predicted reaction times. Water remains present during hydrolysis rather than assuming that complete drying promotes it."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide-assisted attack on the nitrile followed by proton transfers gives prolinamide. Neutral-reference equation: mol_014 + mol_001 -> mol_015.", "reasoning": "Proposed environmental extension. Patel et al. (2015) support the precursor but not alkaline release of free proline. Base-assisted nitrile hydration is chemically conventional, yet sufficiently rapid conversion at pH 10-11 cannot be assumed. This branch tests compatibility with naturally alkaline water and is not ranked above the acidic alternative without kinetic data."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Proposed alkaline surface hydrolysis of prolinamide", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Alkaline aqueous hydroxide reservoir"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Candidate envelope: 350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "OH\u2212-promoted hydrolysis; alkalinity is consumed when carboxylate is retained", "aqueous": true, "other": "Candidate pH 10-11, maintained by continued alkaline inflow or buffering rather than assumed catalytic turnover of a finite amount of base. Product is predominantly prolinate under alkaline conditions. Cooling and subsequent pH equilibration change speciation, not the molecular carbon skeleton. Monitor hydrolysis, decomposition and racemization separately."}, "mechanism": "Base-promoted amide hydrolysis yields prolinate and ammonia; protonation equilibria are outside the molecular reference equation. Neutral-reference equation: 2 mol_015 + 2 mol_001 -> mol_016 + mol_017 + 2 mol_004.", "reasoning": "Proposed extension beyond the aminonitrile endpoint of Patel et al. (2015). The distinction between hydroxide promotion and true catalytic base turnover matters because alkaline amide hydrolysis retains product as carboxylate. Neither the rate under the proposed conditions nor an advantage over acidic processing is established by the supplied literature. This route produces an expected DL inventory, not enantioenriched L-proline."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Proposed moderately hydrothermal aminonitrile hydration", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Acidic hydrothermal proton reservoir"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Candidate envelope: 393-423 K", "pressure": "10-30 bar, maintaining liquid water", "catalyst": "H+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Candidate pH 2-4 in a cooler acidic hydrothermal margin, not the hottest vent endmember. This branch requires physical infiltration or delivery of the surface-made aminonitrile into a hydrothermal circulation path; that transfer is proposed, not demonstrated. Screen residence times from hours to one day and measure precursor survival. No UV is required at depth."}, "mechanism": "Thermally accelerated acid-assisted nitrile hydration gives the same prolinamide intermediate. Reference equation: mol_014 + mol_001 -> mol_015.", "reasoning": "Speculative environmental implementation of conventional nitrile hydration. Patel et al. (2015) supply the defined precursor but do not demonstrate this transfer or hydrolysis regime. Hennet et al. (1992) establish that amino-acid chemistry can operate near 423 K, not that this particular nitrile hydrolyzes selectively there. Higher temperature may improve hydration while increasing destructive side reactions; both fluxes require measurement."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Proposed hydrothermal prolinamide hydrolysis with rapid quenching", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Acidic hydrothermal proton reservoir"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Candidate reaction envelope: 393-423 K; quench outlet to below 320 K", "pressure": "10-30 bar during the hot aqueous stage", "catalyst": "H+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Candidate pH 2-4. Use a flowing moderate-temperature zone, measure the time of maximum free-proline recovery, and mix the outlet promptly with cooler anoxic water. Hours-to-one-day residence times are a screening envelope only. Independently supplied H2-rich surroundings may reduce some degradation but are not assumed to make proline indefinitely stable."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis of the amide side group releases ammonia and the amino-acid carboxyl functionality. Reference equation: 2 mol_015 + 2 mol_001 -> mol_016 + mol_017 + 2 mol_004. Quenching is a physical processing requirement, not an additional synthetic reaction.", "reasoning": "Proposed extension beyond Patel et al. (2015). Bada, Miller and Zhao (1995) demonstrate the importance of irreversible amino-acid destruction at substantially higher temperatures; Yanagawa and Kobayashi (1997) show that hydrogen fugacity can alter decomposition behavior. These studies justify testing short residence times and reducing conditions, not assuming a measured proline yield or a validated hydrothermal advantage."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Empirical reducing-atmosphere discharge and aqueous recovery module", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_019", "mol_004", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_020"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 300-370 K in the collection water; discharge channels are transient nonthermal high-energy regions", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "CH4/N2/H2O with trace ammonia and electrical discharge, followed by collection in water. Assign the setting to a transient impact-reduced or locally reduced atmosphere, not a persistent global methane atmosphere. The supplied report does not specify all original run durations or workup parameters. Record silicate vessel or shoreline contact explicitly, because the surface contribution is not established to be zero."}, "mechanism": "Unresolved radical, carbonyl, nitrogen-fixation and ring-construction network, lumped with aqueous recovery. No ordinary-Strecker-only mechanism is asserted. A balanced target-channel reference is: 20 mol_018 + mol_019 + 2 mol_004 + 8 mol_001 -> 2 mol_016 + 2 mol_017 + 33 mol_020. This is not the measured bulk gas balance, nitrogen-source partition, yield or coproduct inventory.", "reasoning": "Ring et al. (1972) detected proline, reported at approximately 1.5 micromol, in a complex discharge product mixture. Criado-Reyes et al. (2021) show that silicate-containing reactor surfaces can substantially change discharge chemistry, without establishing a specific proline catalyst here. Trail, Watson and Tailby (2011) challenge a persistently strongly reducing atmosphere. The empirical endpoint is supported; the reference equation is a formally balanced projection, and L:D equality is an achiral expectation rather than an enantiomer-resolved result."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Empirical hot formaldehyde-ammonia synthesis and hydrolytic recovery", "inputs": ["mol_021", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_023", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Acid used only in the separate analytical hydrolysis workup"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Reported synthesis temperature approximately 458 K; cool before separate hydrolytic workup", "pressure": "Candidate aqueous-retention envelope: 15-25 bar at 458 K; original pressure is not specified in the supplied summary", "catalyst": "None for the thermal synthesis; H+ during analytical hydrolysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated formaldehyde/ammonia in a closed hot aqueous system. The supplied report does not provide enough information to assign exact feed concentrations, residence time or original hydrolysis conditions. A separate strong-acid laboratory hydrolysis benchmark must therefore be documented explicitly rather than silently modeled as a natural pond process. Cool and dilute outflow before prolonged amino-acid exposure to heat."}, "mechanism": "Empirical condensation/disproportionation chemistry builds unidentified hydrolysable material that releases proline during workup. No molecular identity is assigned to the unresolved bulk precursor. A balanced reference projection is: 12 mol_021 + 2 mol_004 -> mol_016 + mol_017 + 2 mol_023 + 4 mol_001. Formic acid is a proposed formaldehyde-oxidation coproduct, not an experimentally established stoichiometric product of this proline branch.", "reasoning": "Fox and Windsor (1970) report proline after hydrolysis of heated formaldehyde/ammonia products. Their experiment supports laboratory recovery, not a mapped C5-ring mechanism or autonomous environmental free-proline accumulation. Wolman et al. (1971) and the historical acid-blank concerns motivate rigorous blanks and isotope tracing. The formate closure is explicitly speculative: it supplies a chemically plausible disproportionation balance but cannot establish actual reaction flux or product distribution."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Empirical lower-molybdate formaldehyde-hydroxylamine and hydrolysis module", "inputs": ["mol_021", "mol_022"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_023", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Molybdate, 1e-4 M nominal concentration", "Kaolinite, approximately 2% w/v", "Montmorillonite, approximately 2% w/v", "Zn2+, Fe3+, Cu2+, Co2+ and Mn2+, each nominally 1e-4 M in the experimental medium", "Acid used in the separate analytical hydrolysis workup"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Reported synthesis: 378 \u00b1 5 K for seven days", "pressure": "Sealed under N2; candidate geologic analogue 5-10 bar; original total pressure is not supplied", "catalyst": "Empirical multicomponent metal/clay selectivity medium; no unique active catalyst established", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported starting conditions: 0.30 M formaldehyde, 0.05 M hydroxylamine and initial pH 5.5, followed by strong-acid analytical hydrolysis. The 1e-4 M molybdate condition is used rather than the much more problematic 1e-2 M maximum-selectivity condition. A confined hydrothermal pore is the proposed temperature-pressure analogue; an open ambient-pressure pool at 378 K is not assumed. Fe3+ denotes the nominal laboratory addition, not a persistent ferric ocean reservoir; its rapid reaction in a reducing, Fe2+-rich setting must be tested."}, "mechanism": "Unresolved oxime, condensation and possible cyanide chemistry generates hydrolysable proline-bearing material. The empirical metal/clay medium changes selectivity, but the specific ring-forming mechanism is unknown. Balanced reference projection: 14 mol_021 + 2 mol_022 -> mol_016 + mol_017 + 4 mol_023 + 4 mol_001. Formate is a proposed redox-balance coproduct, not a measured branch-specific material balance.", "reasoning": "Hatanaka and Egami (1977, Journal of Biochemistry; 1977, Bulletin of the Chemical Society of Japan) support this experimental family; the reported proline fraction at 1e-4 M molybdate is about 0.16 mol% of analyzed residues, not the 2.75 mol% associated with 1e-2 M. Scott et al. (2008) make even the lower molybdate inventory a geochemical enrichment problem. Hydroxylamine production, reagent concentration, persistence of the nominal mixed-metal medium, and natural replacement of strong-acid workup remain unresolved. This is a conditional laboratory-supported branch, not a demonstrated closed Hadean synthesis."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "Empirical reducing hydrothermal cyanide-carbonyl-ammonium module", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_021", "mol_004", "mol_020"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite assemblage as an experimental redox buffer and mineral interface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Reported synthesis approximately 423 K; proposed outlet quench below 320 K", "pressure": "Reported approximately 10 bar", "catalyst": "Pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite redox-buffering assemblage; a specific proline-forming catalytic site is not established", "aqueous": true, "other": "HCN/KCN, formaldehyde and NH4Cl in reducing hot water. NH3 is the neutral reference node for total ammoniacal nitrogen; actual NH3/NH4+ speciation depends on in situ pH and temperature. The experiments used highly concentrated organics, described in the supplied report as tenths-of-molar-scale rather than natural vent-fluid measurements. Exact run-specific concentrations, pH, H2 fugacity and residence time must be taken from the original protocol, not invented. The proposed natural implementation uses independently supplied serpentinization H2, a moderate-temperature reaction zone and prompt cold-water mixing; it does not require transfer of discharge-produced H2."}, "mechanism": "Empirical hot cyanide/carbonyl/ammonium network with unresolved proline ring construction. A balanced target-channel projection is: mol_002 + 9 mol_021 + mol_004 + 3 mol_020 -> mol_016 + mol_017 + 5 mol_001. This equation is neither the measured bulk material balance nor a demonstrated elementary Strecker sequence, and it does not establish nitrogen atom provenance.", "reasoning": "Hennet, Holm and Engel (1992) detected proline under simulated hydrothermal conditions and explicitly did not claim that their precursor concentrations represented natural fluids. Bada, Miller and Zhao (1995) require inclusion of thermal destruction; Yanagawa and Kobayashi (1997) motivate examining hydrogen fugacity rather than assuming identical degradation in all redox regimes. The mineral assemblage is justified as the experimental environmental buffer, not as an invented selective cyclization catalyst. Feedstock capture, actual residence-time-dependent yield and survival during mixing remain unquantified."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Copper-assisted cyanosulfidic scaffold with acidic surface release", "description": "The best-resolved scaffold sequence reaches the experimentally demonstrated proline aminonitrile, then uses proposed acidic hydration and amide hydrolysis in a separate warm surface acid reservoir. Product is an expected DL inventory containing L-proline. The final hydrolysis sequence, sulfur speciation and autonomous reagent ordering remain unvalidated.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_014", "mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Copper-assisted cyanosulfidic scaffold with alkaline surface release", "description": "Shares the demonstrated scaffold chemistry with P1 but processes the final aminonitrile in an alkaline warm-water reservoir. This is a process alternative, not an independent scaffold demonstration. Hydrolysis at environmentally accessible alkalinity may be slow, and no acid-versus-base yield advantage is asserted.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_012", "mol_014", "mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Copper-assisted surface scaffold with moderately hydrothermal release", "description": "Transfers the completed surface aminonitrile inventory into a moderately hot acidic aqueous circulation zone for proposed hydration and amide hydrolysis, followed by quenching. The required surface-to-subsurface transfer is an explicit integration gap. Faster hydrolysis cannot be assumed to outweigh thermal losses without kinetic measurements.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_014", "rxn_015"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_013", "mol_014", "mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Copper-omitted aminoaldehyde photoreduction with acidic surface release", "description": "Replaces only the aminopropionitrile photoreduction with the explicitly speculative copper-omitted variant, then follows the shared ring-construction sequence and acidic surface hydrolysis. Copper remains involved in upstream acrylonitrile formation. This path tests catalyst dependence and is not an independently demonstrated synthesis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_014", "mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Copper-omitted aminoaldehyde photoreduction with alkaline surface release", "description": "Combines the substrate-specific copper-omission hypothesis with alkaline hydration and amide hydrolysis. Both the copper-free conversion efficiency at rxn_004 and the final alkaline release rate require demonstration. The downstream ring chemistry does not validate either extension.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_012", "mol_014", "mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Copper-omitted aminoaldehyde photoreduction with hydrothermal release", "description": "Combines the proposed copper-omitted photoreduction stage with the surface scaffold sequence and a proposed moderate-temperature hydrothermal hydrolysis outlet. It shares the chemical evidence of P1-P5 and adds no independent experimental validation. Copper carryover, transport, hydrolysis and degradation are separate bottlenecks.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_014", "rxn_015"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_013", "mol_014", "mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Transient reducing-atmosphere electrical-discharge route", "description": "Empirically supported access to proline from simple gases and water, represented without inventing a ring-forming intermediate. The low-abundance laboratory endpoint does not establish a natural production flux, a global reducing atmosphere, a complete coproduct balance or L-selectivity.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_016"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Thermal formaldehyde-ammonia hydrolysate route", "description": "Hot aqueous formaldehyde/ammonia chemistry yields material from which proline is recovered after hydrolysis. This empirical synthesis-and-workup module avoids naming an uncharacterized precursor as a molecule. Its major gaps are natural concentration, precursor identity, redox coproducts and replacement of analytical hydrolysis by an environmental release process.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Lower-molybdate formaldehyde-hydroxylamine hydrolysate route", "description": "Uses the reported 1e-4 M molybdate member of the multicomponent metal/clay experiment rather than importing the 10 mM maximum-selectivity result. Proline recovery is experimentally supported at the selected condition, but hydroxylamine supply, local molybdate enrichment, ferric-metal persistence and natural hydrolysis remain unresolved.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_018"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Reducing hydrothermal cyanide-carbonyl-ammonium route", "description": "Empirically supported hydrothermal access to proline at approximately 423 K and 10 bar with a justified Fe-S/oxide redox assemblage. The ring-forming mechanism and natural precursor activities are unresolved. Independently available hydrothermal H2 and rapid quenching are proposed environmental requirements, not demonstrated closure of the natural synthesis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_019"], "key_intermediates": []}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_004", "mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_009", "mol_014", "mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_021", "mol_023"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_009", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_005"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_015", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_010", "rxn_012", "rxn_014"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_011", "rxn_013", "rxn_015"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_016", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_011", "rxn_013", "rxn_015", "rxn_016", "rxn_017", "rxn_018", "rxn_019"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_017", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_011", "rxn_013", "rxn_015", "rxn_016", "rxn_017", "rxn_018", "rxn_019"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_006", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_023", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_017", "rxn_018"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 543761.5411281586, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T06:42:48.518694", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: L-Proline\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct the proline skeleton through five literature-supported route families: the structurally resolved cyanosulfidic sequence, electrical-discharge chemistry, thermal formaldehyde/ammonia chemistry, formaldehyde/hydroxylamine chemistry, and hydrothermal cyanide/carbonyl/ammonium chemistry. The ten listed pathways are distinct process-level reaction sequences, not ten independently demonstrated scaffold syntheses: P1-P6 combine one cyanosulfidic scaffold sequence with alternative photoreduction and hydrolysis conditions; P7-P10 are empirical synthesis-and-recovery modules. No surveyed study establishes an autonomous, geochemically integrated synthesis of predominantly L-proline. Accordingly, L-proline and D-proline are explicit coproducts, with an expected racemic distribution rather than an asserted measured enantiomeric ratio. No unsupported resolution step is included. Patel et al. (2015) establish the cyclic aminonitrile endpoint; its environmental conversion to free proline remains a proposed extension. Numerical operating envelopes identified as candidate conditions are testable proposals, not reported experimental protocols or measured environmental concentrations. Historical endpoint experiments are represented as explicitly lumped modules because their ring-forming intermediates are unresolved; analytical acid hydrolysis is not equated with natural monomer availability. Reference equations in mechanism fields ensure elemental balance and an explicitly symmetric L:D representation, but empirical-module coefficients and sulfur/formate coproduct assignments are not measured process balances. Molecular formulas use neutral reference forms; aqueous protonation equilibria are implicit. Stereounspecified chiral intermediates represent unresolved enantiomeric inventories. The restricted role vocabulary requires terminal coproducts to be labeled intermediate; mol_006, mol_017 and mol_023 are coproduct reservoirs, not obligatory downstream intermediates. A null environment_formed denotes an externally supplied species or a species formed in multiple environments. Extraterrestrial ice and irradiated-formamide routes are excluded because their demonstrated conditions do not fit the permitted terrestrial environments. The especially problematic 10 mM molybdate condition is also excluded.\n\nMOLECULES (23 nodes):\n  - mol_001: H2O (Water) [starting]\n  - mol_002: CHN (Hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_003: C2H2 (Acetylene) [starting]\n  - mol_004: NH3 (Ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_005: H2S (Hydrogen sulfide; neutral reference for aqueous H2S/HS\u2212) [starting]\n  - mol_006: S8 (Elemental sulfur; terminal sulfur-balance reference) [intermediate]\n  - mol_007: C3H3N (Acrylonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_008: C3H6N2 (Beta-aminopropionitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_009: C3H7NO (Beta-aminopropionaldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_010: C4H8N2O (3-Aminopropanal cyanohydrin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_011: C4H10N2OS (Acyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C4H7NOS (Cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C4H7NS (Gamma-butyrothiolactam) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C5H8N2 (Proline aminonitrile; stereounspecified inventory) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_015: C5H10N2O (Prolinamide; stereounspecified inventory) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_016: C5H9NO2 (L-Proline) [target]\n  - mol_017: C5H9NO2 (D-Proline) [intermediate]\n  - mol_018: CH4 (Methane) [starting]\n  - mol_019: N2 (Molecular nitrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_020: H2 (Molecular hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_021: CH2O (Formaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_022: H3NO (Hydroxylamine) [starting]\n  - mol_023: CH2O2 (Formic acid; proposed disproportionation coproduct) [intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (19 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_002, mol_003 -> mol_007 (Copper-assisted acetylene hydrocyanation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_007, mol_004 -> mol_008 (Ammonia conjugate addition to acrylonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_008, mol_005, mol_001 -> mol_009, mol_004, mol_006 (Copper-assisted sulfide photoreduction to 3-aminopropanal) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_008, mol_005, mol_001 -> mol_009, mol_004, mol_006 (Conditional copper-omitted sulfide photoreduction) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_009, mol_002 -> mol_010 (Cyanohydrin homologation of 3-aminopropanal) [Surface]\n  - rxn_006: mol_010, mol_005 -> mol_011 (Sulfide addition to the cyanohydrin nitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_007: mol_011 -> mol_012, mol_004 (Five-membered thiolactam ring formation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol_012, mol_005 -> mol_013, mol_001, mol_006 (Photoreductive deoxygenation to gamma-butyrothiolactam) [Surface]\n  - rxn_009: mol_013, mol_005, mol_002 -> mol_014, mol_005, mol_006 (Thiolactam photoreduction followed by cyanide trapping) [Surface]\n  - rxn_010: mol_014, mol_001 -> mol_015 (Proposed acidic surface hydration of proline aminonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_011: mol_015, mol_001 -> mol_016, mol_017, mol_004 (Proposed acidic surface hydrolysis of prolinamide) [Surface]\n  - rxn_012: mol_014, mol_001 -> mol_015 (Proposed alkaline surface hydration of proline aminonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_013: mol_015, mol_001 -> mol_016, mol_017, mol_004 (Proposed alkaline surface hydrolysis of prolinamide) [Surface]\n  - rxn_014: mol_014, mol_001 -> mol_015 (Proposed moderately hydrothermal aminonitrile hydration) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_015: mol_015, mol_001 -> mol_016, mol_017, mol_004 (Proposed hydrothermal prolinamide hydrolysis with rapid quenching) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_016: mol_018, mol_019, mol_004, mol_001 -> mol_016, mol_017, mol_020 (Empirical reducing-atmosphere discharge and aqueous recovery module) [Surface]\n  - rxn_017: mol_021, mol_004 -> mol_016, mol_017, mol_023, mol_001 (Empirical hot formaldehyde-ammonia synthesis and hydrolytic recovery) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_018: mol_021, mol_022 -> mol_016, mol_017, mol_023, mol_001 (Empirical lower-molybdate formaldehyde-hydroxylamine and hydrolysis module) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_019: mol_002, mol_021, mol_004, mol_020 -> mol_016, mol_017, mol_001 (Empirical reducing hydrothermal cyanide-carbonyl-ammonium module) [Hydrothermal]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Copper-assisted cyanosulfidic scaffold with acidic surface release (10 steps)\n  - P2: Copper-assisted cyanosulfidic scaffold with alkaline surface release (10 steps)\n  - P3: Copper-assisted surface scaffold with moderately hydrothermal release (10 steps)\n  - P4: Copper-omitted aminoaldehyde photoreduction with acidic surface release (10 steps)\n  - P5: Copper-omitted aminoaldehyde photoreduction with alkaline surface release (10 steps)\n  - P6: Copper-omitted aminoaldehyde photoreduction with hydrothermal release (10 steps)\n  - P7: Transient reducing-atmosphere electrical-discharge route (1 steps)\n  - P8: Thermal formaldehyde-ammonia hydrolysate route (1 steps)\n  - P9: Lower-molybdate formaldehyde-hydroxylamine hydrolysate route (1 steps)\n  - P10: Reducing hydrothermal cyanide-carbonyl-ammonium route (1 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 19 reactions | 8 balanced as written | 11 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_003 balanceable: 8 C3H6N2 + 8 H2S + 8 H2O -> 8 C3H7NO + 8 NH3 + S8\n  - rxn_004 balanceable: 8 C3H6N2 + 8 H2S + 8 H2O -> 8 C3H7NO + 8 NH3 + S8\n  - rxn_008 balanceable: 8 C4H7NOS + 8 H2S -> 8 C4H7NS + 8 H2O + S8\n  - rxn_009 balanceable: 8 C4H7NS + H2S + 8 CHN -> 8 C5H8N2 + H2S + S8\n  - rxn_011 balanceable: 2 C5H10N2O + 2 H2O -> C5H9NO2 + C5H9NO2 + 2 NH3\n  - rxn_013 balanceable: 2 C5H10N2O + 2 H2O -> C5H9NO2 + C5H9NO2 + 2 NH3\n  - rxn_015 balanceable: 2 C5H10N2O + 2 H2O -> C5H9NO2 + C5H9NO2 + 2 NH3\n  - rxn_016 balanceable: 15 CH4 + N2 + NH3 + 6 H2O -> 2 C5H9NO2 + C5H9NO2 + 24 H2\n  - rxn_017 balanceable: 12 CH2O + 2 NH3 -> C5H9NO2 + C5H9NO2 + 2 CH2O2 + 4 H2O\n  - rxn_018 balanceable: 14 CH2O + 2 H3NO -> C5H9NO2 + C5H9NO2 + 4 CH2O2 + 4 H2O\n  - rxn_019 balanceable: CHN + 9 CH2O + NH3 + 3 H2 -> C5H9NO2 + C5H9NO2 + 5 H2O\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH ON TARGET MOLECULE ===\nThe following research was conducted on the target molecule before network generation. Use it to assess whether the network aligns with known prebiotic chemistry literature.\n\n=== DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of L\u2011Proline: Experimental Routes, Mechanisms, Geochemical Context, and Open Problems\n\n## Executive assessment\n\nThe primary literature supports **multiple chemically distinct routes to the proline carbon\u2013nitrogen skeleton**, but it does **not** yet provide a compelling prebiotic synthesis of specifically **L-proline** from simple achiral feedstocks with significant enantiomeric selectivity. In most papers the product is reported simply as \u201cproline,\u201d is explicitly racemic, or was not subjected to enantiomer-resolved analysis. Thus, for origin-of-life network design, it is important to distinguish **prebiotic synthesis of the proline scaffold** from **prebiotic production of enantioenriched L-proline**. The former has substantial experimental precedent; the latter remains unsolved. Mu\u00f1oz Caro and co-workers' interstellar-ice experiments yielded chiral amino acids without a biological handedness, while meteoritic studies resolve both proline enantiomers rather than demonstrating an abiotic L-selective source.\n\nThe experimental literature I could identify falls into **seven major families** relevant to proline: electric-discharge atmospheric synthesis; thermal formaldehyde\u2013ammonia chemistry; formaldehyde\u2013hydroxylamine chemistry in metal/clay media; hydrothermal HCN\u2013formaldehyde\u2013ammonium chemistry; the cyanosulfidic photoredox route of Patel and co-workers; formamide chemistry driven by particle irradiation and meteoritic minerals; and UV photochemistry of astrophysical ice analogues. Of these, some give **proline itself only after hydrolysis**, some report proline in complex product mixtures without a resolved mechanism, and the mechanistically most detailed modern route\u2014Patel et al. (2015)\u2014stops experimentally at the **\u03b1-aminonitrile precursor of proline**, rather than reporting an isolated yield of free proline.\n\nThe strongest conclusions are therefore:\n\n| Route family | What is experimentally established for proline? | Principal strength | Principal weakness |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Electric discharge | Proline detected in a CH\u2084/N\u2082/H\u2082O/trace-NH\u2083 discharge product mixture | Very simple starting gases; classic direct experiment | Low proline abundance; mechanism unresolved; globally reducing atmosphere contested |\n| HCHO + NH\u2083, heat | Proline appears after hydrolysis of the thermal product | No mineral catalyst required | Product is a hydrolysate; high temperature and poorly resolved C\u2085-ring mechanism |\n| HCHO + NH\u2082OH + metals/clays/MoO\u2084\u00b2\u207b | Proline selectively enhanced, reaching 2.75 mol% of analyzed amino-acid residues at 10\u207b\u00b2 M molybdate | Strongest proline-specific selectivity experiment | Extremely concentrated HCHO/NH\u2082OH and molybdate; many simultaneous catalysts; acid hydrolysis |\n| Hydrothermal HCN/HCHO/NH\u2084\u207a | Proline detected among hydrothermally generated amino acids at 150 \u00b0C | Demonstrates compatibility with reducing hydrothermal mineral assemblages | Starting organics are very concentrated; authors did not claim those concentrations were natural; thermal destruction competes |\n| Cyanosulfidic photoredox | Explicit pathway to pyrrolidine \u03b1-aminonitrile, the precursor of proline | Best-resolved proline-specific mechanism | Multi-step, requires HCN/acetylene/HS\u207b/UV and Cu chemistry; free proline not the demonstrated endpoint |\n| Formamide + meteorite + proton irradiation | Proline directly identified in irradiated products | Independent C\u2081 feedstock framework; broad chemical inventory | Uses neat formamide and high-energy irradiation; proline-specific mechanism unresolved |\n| Interstellar-ice UV chemistry | Proline among amino acids released from irradiated ice residues | Plausible extraterrestrial synthesis and delivery framework | Often detected after warm-up/hydrolysis; not a terrestrial synthesis; racemic/no L-selectivity |\n\nThese routes are **not mutually exclusive**. Atmospheric photochemistry could make HCN and carbonyl precursors later processed in ponds or hydrothermal systems; extraterrestrial material could supplement terrestrial inventories; and the same HCN, formaldehyde, ammonia and reduced sulfur chemistry recur in several otherwise competing frameworks. Conversely, the recurrence of a reagent in laboratory networks does not establish that all of the required compounds were co-located on the Hadean Earth at the concentrations used experimentally.\n\n## Known synthesis routes and experimental evidence\n\n### Electric-discharge synthesis from reduced atmospheric gases\n\nDavid Ring, Yecheskel Wolman, Nadav Friedmann and Stanley Miller reported one of the clearest early observations of abiotic proline in **1972 in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences**. Their electric-discharge experiments operated on **methane, molecular nitrogen and water with traces of ammonia**. Ion-exchange chromatography and gas chromatography\u2013mass spectrometry confirmed a broad amino-acid suite including glycine, alanine, branched-chain amino acids, aspartate, glutamate, serine, threonine and **proline**.\n\nThe original product table reports proline at only about **1.5 \u03bcmol**, far below glycine and alanine. That low abundance is chemically informative: the discharge robustly produces simple Strecker-type amino-acid chemistry, but construction of proline's five-membered N-heterocycle is a minor branch of the reaction network rather than a dominant pathway. Ring et al. did not establish a unique sequence of elementary reactions leading specifically to proline.\n\nA reasonable network interpretation is that the discharge first creates reactive C\u2081\u2013C\u2083 species\u2014HCN, carbonyl compounds and other reduced nitrogen compounds\u2014from which more complex amino-acid precursors arise. It would, however, be an overstatement to call the Ring proline synthesis a demonstrated \u201cordinary Strecker synthesis.\u201d A standard aldehyde + NH\u2083 + HCN Strecker sequence produces an **acyclic \u03b1-aminonitrile**; proline additionally requires formation of the pyrrolidine ring. Ring et al. detected the endpoint but did not resolve that ring-forming step.\n\nThere is also an experimentally demonstrated **surface complication** in interpreting Miller-type chemistry. Joaqu\u00edn Criado-Reyes, Bruno Bizzarri, Juan Manuel Garc\u00eda-Ruiz, Raffaele Saladino and Ernesto Di Mauro showed in **2021 in Scientific Reports** that otherwise similar discharge experiments behave very differently in borosilicate versus Teflon vessels: borosilicate increases both molecular diversity and yields of several classes of products. Their experiments used CH\u2084/N\u2082/NH\u2083, electrical discharge, pH about 11 when unbuffered or pH 8.7 with NH\u2084Cl, and deliberately compared glass, Teflon and Teflon containing glass fragments. Thus \u201cno added mineral catalyst\u201d in old discharge experiments should not necessarily be read as \u201cno catalytic surface.\u201d\n\nThe largest environmental objection is the gas mixture. Dustin Trail, E. Bruce Watson and Nicholas Tailby reported in **2011 in Nature** that redox-sensitive Ce systematics in Hadean zircons imply mantle-derived melts near the fayalite\u2013magnetite\u2013quartz buffer by roughly 4.35 Ga. Such magmas would have outgassed predominantly H\u2082O, CO\u2082, N\u2082 and oxidized sulfur rather than the globally CH\u2084/NH\u2083-rich atmosphere assumed in strongly reducing Miller experiments. This makes a **globally persistent Ring-type atmosphere contested**, though it does not exclude transient impact-generated or locally reduced volcanic environments.\n\nThat critique does not imply that atmospheric HCN was impossible. Sarah Rugheimer and Paul Rimmer showed in **2019 in Icarus**, using atmospheric photochemical modeling, that HCN production can occur in nitrogen-rich atmospheres even without methane, with its abundance strongly controlled by destruction chemistry. Consequently, the atmospheric objection is strongest against the *specific highly reducing bulk gas composition*, not against every atmospheric route to HCN-based prebiotic chemistry.\n\n**Assessment:** experimentally secure production of proline, but low-yield, mechanistically under-resolved and geochemically dependent on a debated atmospheric redox regime.\n\n### Thermal formaldehyde\u2013ammonia chemistry\n\nSidney Fox and Charles Windsor reported in **1970 in Science** that heating **formaldehyde and ammonia** gives a product which, after hydrolysis, yields seven identified amino acids: aspartic acid, glutamic acid, serine, **proline**, valine, glycine and alanine, with glycine predominant. Their experiment is significant because no mineral catalyst, cyanide addition or electrical discharge was required once HCHO and NH\u2083 were supplied.\n\nThe reaction was performed at approximately **185 \u00b0C** in an aqueous formaldehyde/ammonia system. Fox and Windsor interpreted these conditions both cosmochemically and in terms of hot terrestrial environments. What they demonstrated analytically, however, was principally the formation of **hydrolysable material that releases amino acids** rather than quantitative accumulation of free proline in the unhydrolyzed reaction mixture.\n\nThat distinction is especially important for proline. Formaldehyde supplies only one carbon atom at a time, so a C\u2085 proline framework must arise by extensive carbon\u2013carbon bond-forming condensation chemistry before ring closure. Fox and Windsor did not establish which C\u2082\u2013C\u2085 carbonyl, nitrile, imine or polymeric intermediate gives proline. The experiment therefore establishes a **route family**, not a resolved reaction mechanism.\n\nWolman, Miller, Ib\u00e1\u00f1ez and Or\u00f3 revisited formaldehyde/ammonia as prebiotic amino-acid precursors in **1971 in Science**, illustrating that this chemistry was already controversial enough to generate immediate experimental scrutiny. More generally, Wolman and Miller's contemporaneous work on amino-acid contamination in hydrochloric acid highlighted an important analytical issue for very low-yield experiments in which products are only observed after strong-acid hydrolysis. This historical caveat does not demonstrate that Fox and Windsor's proline was contamination, but it means hydrolysis blanks and, ideally, isotope tracing are essential when interpreting such experiments.\n\n**Assessment:** a catalyst-free, chemically simple route to a proline-releasing product, but one of the least mechanistically resolved routes and difficult to translate directly into a natural Hadean microenvironment.\n\n### Formaldehyde\u2013hydroxylamine chemistry with clays, transition metals and molybdate\n\nHiroshi Hatanaka and Fujio Egami produced the most striking early **proline-selectivity result**. In **1977 in the Journal of Biochemistry**, they reacted formaldehyde and hydroxylamine in a modified seawater medium containing transition metals and clays while varying molybdate concentration. Increasing MoO\u2084\u00b2\u207b had little effect on the total concentration of primary amino groups but strongly changed the product distribution, selectively favoring alanine, aspartate, \u03b2-alanine and, most dramatically, **proline**.\n\nTheir standard experiments used approximately **0.30 M formaldehyde and 0.05 M hydroxylamine**, an initial **pH of 5.5**, and heating at **105 \u00b1 5 \u00b0C for seven days** in sealed vessels under N\u2082. The modified sea medium contained **Zn\u00b2\u207a, Fe\u00b3\u207a, Cu\u00b2\u207a, Co\u00b2\u207a and Mn\u00b2\u207a at 10\u207b\u2074 M each**, with MoO\u2084\u00b2\u207b varied through **10\u207b\u2076, 10\u207b\u2074 and 10\u207b\u00b2 M**; kaolin and montmorillonite were generally present at about **2% w/v each**. Products were subjected to strong-acid hydrolysis before amino-acid analysis. Hatanaka and Egami's companion **1977 Bulletin of the Chemical Society of Japan** study reported roughly forty ninhydrin-active amino-acid species or related products in this reaction family and oligomeric material with molecular masses of roughly 200\u20131,000.\n\nThe molybdate dependence is quantitatively remarkable. Proline represented **<0.01 mol%** of the analyzed amino-acid residues at 10\u207b\u2076 M MoO\u2084\u00b2\u207b, about **0.16 mol%** at 10\u207b\u2074 M, and **2.75 mol% at 10\u207b\u00b2 M MoO\u2084\u00b2\u207b**. Glycine moved in the opposite direction, falling from approximately 33.6 to 10.4 mol% over the same molybdate series. Thus molybdate did not simply increase \u201camino-acid production\u201d; it **redirected network selectivity toward more complex products, especially proline**.\n\nMechanistically, this system is not solved. Hatanaka and Egami explicitly left open the possibility of a common intermediate such as **HCN**. Formaldehyde and hydroxylamine readily access oxime chemistry, nitrogen-containing carbon species and ultimately cyanide chemistry under sufficiently vigorous conditions; repeated C\u2013C construction followed by N-heterocycle formation is therefore possible, but no experimentally established sequence from HCHO/NH\u2082OH to the pyrrolidine ring was supplied.\n\nCalling molybdate \u201cthe catalyst\u201d also oversimplifies the experiment. Molybdate concentration was the manipulated variable that altered selectivity, but the reactors simultaneously contained five other transition-metal ions plus kaolinite and montmorillonite. The experiment therefore demonstrates a **molybdate-dependent catalytic/selectivity effect in a multicomponent mineral system**, not a uniquely isolated Mo-catalyzed elementary mechanism.\n\nIts geochemical challenge is severe. Scott, Lyons, Bekker, Shen, Poulton, Chu and Anbar showed in **2008 in Nature** that even long after the Hadean, dissolved-ocean molybdenum inventories remained strongly limited under poorly oxygenated conditions; substantial Mo mobilization is associated with oxidative continental weathering. Anbar and colleagues similarly used late-Archean Mo/Re enrichments as evidence for unusually early episodes of oxidative weathering. Extrapolating those constraints backward, **10 mM molybdate in an anoxic Hadean aqueous setting is difficult to justify as an ordinary seawater concentration**. Local mineral-rich microenvironments are a different question, but the Hatanaka experiment does not demonstrate natural enrichment to that level.\n\nHydroxylamine presents a second feedstock problem. The experiment assumes NH\u2082OH directly rather than demonstrating its production from N\u2082, NH\u2083 or another primary nitrogen reservoir in the same setting. Consequently this route is chemically impressive but requires an upstream **hydroxylamine-generation and concentration mechanism** before it becomes a closed prebiotic network.\n\n**Assessment:** one of the strongest direct demonstrations that mineral/metal composition can greatly favor proline, but its specific reagent concentrations and especially the 10\u207b\u00b2 M molybdate condition are among the least geochemically secure features of any route considered here.\n\n### Hydrothermal HCN\u2013formaldehyde\u2013ammonium chemistry\n\nR. J.-C. Hennet, Nils Holm and Michael Engel reported in **1992 in Naturwissenschaften** that amino acids including **proline** can form under simulated hydrothermal conditions. Their chemistry was essentially a hydrothermal implementation of cyanide/carbonyl/ammonium chemistry in the presence of mineral redox buffers. Experiments were performed around **150 \u00b0C and 10 bar**, with HCN/KCN, formaldehyde and NH\u2084Cl and reducing gas/mineral conditions; pyrite\u2013pyrrhotite\u2013magnetite and, in other experiments, illite were used to impose geologically motivated mineral environments.\n\nA particularly important feature of the Hennet paper is the authors' own restraint about prebiotic relevance: they explicitly did **not** claim that the high cyanide, formaldehyde and ammonium concentrations used in the reactors represented natural hydrothermal fluids. Their central question was whether amino-acid synthesis was chemically possible under hydrothermal temperature, pressure, pH and redox conditions once suitable carbon and nitrogen precursors were present. Proline was a minor product relative to glycine and alanine.\n\nWilliam Marshall expanded this experimental framework in **1994 in Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta**. He generated amino acids and abundant amines from NH\u2084HCO\u2083 with acetylene, H\u2082 and O\u2082 at **200\u2013275 \u00b0C over approximately 0.2\u20132 h**, and also produced amino acids at **210 \u00b0C** from NH\u2084OH, HCHO, NaCN and H\u2082. These experiments reinforce the general point that amino-acid-forming reactions can operate rapidly in hot aqueous systems, although Marshall's paper is broader evidence for hydrothermal amino-acid synthesis rather than an independent proline-specific mechanistic demonstration.\n\nThe strongest published objection is thermal stability. Jeffrey Bada, Stanley Miller and Meixun Zhao reported in **1995 in Origins of Life and Evolution of the Biosphere** that amino acids heated at **240 \u00b0C** are irreversibly destroyed and argued that equilibrium-thermodynamic calculations can seriously overestimate their persistence in hydrothermal systems because kinetic destruction matters. This directly challenges origin scenarios that assume high-temperature vent fluids can accumulate amino acids simply because their nominal formation is thermodynamically favorable.\n\nThe counterpoint is that hydrothermal **redox conditions matter strongly**. Yanagawa and Kobayashi reported in **1997 in Chemistry Letters** that amino-acid decomposition was substantially slower under high hydrogen fugacity than in conventional laboratory heating experiments. Natural vents are also flow systems in which newly synthesized material can be rapidly quenched by mixing with cold seawater, rather than sealed reactors held indefinitely at the maximum vent temperature. Thus the serious high-temperature destruction problem does not by itself rule out all hydrothermal contribution; it shifts attention to **residence time, H\u2082 fugacity, mixing and temperature gradients**.\n\nFor proline specifically, the route remains mechanistically weak compared with Patel's cyanosulfidic pathway. Hennet et al. showed that the endpoint can occur in a hot Strecker-like network, but not which C\u2083/C\u2084 intermediate undergoes the crucial pyrrolidine-ring closure. It therefore provides stronger environmental-condition evidence than mechanism evidence.\n\n**Assessment:** credible proof that proline can emerge from cyanide/carbonyl/ammonium chemistry under hydrothermal conditions, but the experimental precursor concentrations and temperature-dependent destruction make extrapolation to natural vent fluxes highly uncertain.\n\n### Cyanosulfidic photoredox synthesis through a defined proline aminonitrile\n\nBhavesh Patel, Claudia Percivalle, Dougal Ritson, Colm Duffy and John Sutherland reported in **2015 in Nature Chemistry** the most mechanistically explicit modern prebiotic route toward proline. The pathway is part of their \u201ccyanosulfidic protometabolism,\u201d in which HCN and related nitriles undergo UV-driven reductive chemistry using hydrogen sulfide/hydrosulfide, with copper photoredox chemistry accelerating key transformations. Unlike the classic discharge experiments, the route intentionally follows identifiable intermediates into a **cyclic \u03b1-aminonitrile precursor of proline**.\n\nThe proline branch can be summarized as:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{HCN + HC{\\equiv}CH}\n\\rightarrow\n\\mathrm{CH_2{=}CHCN}\n\\rightarrow\n\\mathrm{NH_2CH_2CH_2CN}\n\\rightarrow\n\\mathrm{NH_2CH_2CH_2CHO}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{cyanohydrin}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{cyclic thioamide}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile}\n\\rightarrow\n\\mathrm{Pro}.\n\\]\n\nMore specifically, HCN/acetylene chemistry gives **acrylonitrile**; ammonia adds to acrylonitrile to form **\u03b2-aminopropionitrile**; UV/hydrosulfide reduction produces **\u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde (3-aminopropanal)**; HCN adds to give its cyanohydrin; reaction with HS\u207b forms \u03b1-hydroxythioamide species, one of which cyclizes; irradiation/reduction yields **\u03b3-butyrothiolactam**; further reduction followed by cyanide chemistry gives compound 47, the **\u03b1-aminonitrile precursor of proline**.\n\nThis sequence solves a structural problem that ordinary Strecker chemistry does not: it creates the N-containing five-membered ring **before** final conversion of the nitrile into the carboxyl group. It is therefore much more informative for synthesis-network design than simply observing proline among dozens of discharge products.\n\nThe individual transformations were chemically efficient in the laboratory. Patel et al.'s reported network includes approximately **83% conversion of acrylonitrile to \u03b2-aminopropionitrile**, **45% conversion to \u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde**, essentially quantitative cyanohydrin formation from that aldehyde, substantial formation/cyclization of the corresponding thioamide manifold, and roughly **32% conversion of the cyclic thioamide through the final two-step sequence to the proline aminonitrile**. These should not be multiplied naively into an \u201coverall prebiotic yield,\u201d because the yields were obtained for separately optimized network segments rather than an uninterrupted natural one-pot sequence.\n\nThe distinction between **proline and its aminonitrile precursor** is crucial. Patel et al.'s central experimental endpoint is compound 47, not an isolated quantified sample of free proline generated under the same cyanosulfidic conditions. Conversion of an \u03b1-aminonitrile to the corresponding amino acid by hydrolysis is chemically standard, but the paper's evidence is therefore properly described as a **demonstrated prebiotic route to the immediate proline precursor**, rather than a complete measured free-proline synthesis.\n\nCatalysis is nuanced in this framework. Copper(I)/copper(II) chemistry facilitates important nitrile-generating and photoredox transformations, while **HS\u207b acts as a stoichiometric reductant**. Patel et al. also found that some photoreductions proceed with sulfide even when Cu(I) is not essential, so \u201ccopper-catalyzed synthesis\u201d is not an adequate description of the entire route. UV light is the energetic driver for the photochemical stages.\n\nIts prebiotic challenge is not that any individual reaction is implausible, but that the pathway requires an environmental sequence supplying and managing **HCN, acetylene, ammonia, hydrosulfide, copper species, ultraviolet light and suitable pH/redox conditions** without destructive side reactions. The chemistry is strongest as a connected laboratory reaction network; whether a natural Hadean watershed or pond could reproduce the necessary concentration and temporal ordering remains contested. Atmospheric modeling by Rugheimer and Rimmer supports the possibility of HCN supply even in some non-methane-dominated atmospheres, but that does not automatically solve acetylene, sulfide, copper or concentration requirements.\n\nThe route is also **not congruent with modern proline biosynthesis** in the narrow sense of reproducing the extant glutamate/semialdehyde/pyrroline pathway. Its nitrile/thioamide chemistry is an abiotic alternative. That lack of biochemical congruence is a legitimate criterion in frameworks that expect metabolism to inherit its earliest chemistry, but it is not itself experimental evidence against the route: other origin models explicitly allow biochemical pathways to replace earlier environmental chemistry.\n\n**Assessment:** currently the best-resolved chemical mechanism leading specifically to the proline skeleton from small prebiotic feedstocks, but its endpoint is a proline aminonitrile, and environmental integration of the many required reagents remains the main unresolved issue.\n\n### Formamide chemistry catalyzed by meteorites under particle irradiation\n\nRaffaele Saladino, Eleonora Carota, Giorgia Botta, Mikhail Kapralov, Gennady Timoshenko, Alexei Rozanov, Eugene Krasavin and Ernesto Di Mauro reported in **2015 in Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences** that **liquid formamide** irradiated with high-energy protons in the presence of powdered meteorites produces an unusually broad suite of prebiotic products, including glycine, alanine and **proline**. Meteorites from the major iron, stony-iron, chondrite and achondrite classes were examined.\n\nThe experiments used neat formamide mixed with meteorite powder and irradiated at approximately **243 K** with **170 MeV protons for about three minutes**, at an absorbed dose of roughly **6 Gy**. Controls lacking radiation, formamide or catalyst were included. Proline was noteworthy because the authors reported that it had **not previously been detected in their thermal formamide-condensation chemistry**; its appearance was therefore associated with the irradiation/meteorite regime rather than simply hot formamide chemistry.\n\nFormamide irradiation generates species including **HCN, NH\u2083, CO/CO\u2082 and radical carbon/nitrogen intermediates**. Saladino et al. proposed that amino-acid formation can involve Strecker-like aminonitrile chemistry, while also emphasizing radical pathways for products that ordinary Strecker chemistry cannot explain. For proline specifically, the mechanistic assignment remains substantially weaker than Patel's pathway: a generic Strecker proposal does not by itself explain formation of the pyrrolidine ring.\n\nMeteorite powder is a genuine catalytic/surface variable in this experiment: product distributions changed with meteoritic class, and controls showed that irradiation alone was not equivalent to irradiated formamide plus meteoritic material. The proposed environment is principally **extraterrestrial\u2014irradiated asteroid, meteoroid or other small-body material\u2014rather than an ordinary terrestrial pond**.\n\nThe route's largest prebiotic-relevance problem is the use of **neat formamide**. Detection of formamide in astrophysical settings demonstrates availability, not the natural occurrence of macroscopic reservoirs of essentially pure liquid formamide. A second open methodological issue is source attribution for low-abundance biomolecules such as proline: the study used extensive controls, but isotope-labeled formamide tracing of the carbon and nitrogen entering each amino acid would provide an especially stringent confirmation in future replications. That is a methodological inference rather than evidence that the reported proline is artifactual.\n\n**Assessment:** a genuine experimentally demonstrated alternative framework for proline production from a C\u2081 nitrogen\u2013carbon feedstock, strongest as an exogenous irradiation scenario and weakest in explaining natural formamide concentration and the proline ring-forming mechanism.\n\n### Ultraviolet synthesis in interstellar/pre-cometary ice analogues\n\nG. M. Mu\u00f1oz Caro, U. J. Meierhenrich, W. A. Schutte, B. Barbier, A. Arcones Segovia, H. Rosenbauer, W. H.-P. Thiemann, A. Brack and J. M. Greenberg reported in **2002 in Nature** that UV irradiation of an interstellar-ice analogue at about **12 K under high vacuum** produced a residue containing **16 amino acids after warming and analytical workup**, including proline. Their isotopically labeled carbon-bearing starting materials were chosen to distinguish newly synthesized material from terrestrial contamination.\n\nThe feedstock family consisted of astrophysically relevant simple ice components such as **H\u2082O, CH\u2083OH, NH\u2083, CO and CO\u2082**. UV photons, rather than mineral catalysis, drive radical generation in the solid ice. Warm-up allows radicals and photoproducts trapped in the ice matrix to recombine, producing increasingly complex refractory organic material.\n\nAn independent 2002 **Nature** experiment by Max Bernstein, Jason Dworkin, Scott Sandford, George Cooper and Louis Allamandola used UV-irradiated ice containing H\u2082O, NH\u2083, CH\u2083OH and HCN and robustly demonstrated racemic glycine, alanine and serine. That experiment did **not** itself establish proline, but it is important independent evidence that amino-acid synthesis from irradiated astrophysical ice is reproducible as a general phenomenon rather than unique to the Mu\u00f1oz Caro setup.\n\nFor network interpretation, one caveat is decisive: amino acids detected after ice irradiation and **acid hydrolysis** need not have existed entirely as free amino-acid monomers in the cold ice. Some can be stored as aminonitriles or incorporated into refractory organic precursors that release amino acids during laboratory hydrolysis. Thus the observation is compelling evidence that the ice chemistry generates the **molecular precursors of proline**, but is less direct evidence for a large pre-existing free-proline inventory in interstellar ice.\n\nThe extraterrestrial argument is strengthened independently by meteorites. Michael Engel and Bartholomew Nagy reported in **1982 in Nature** enantiomer-resolved **proline in an interior sample of the Murchison meteorite**, together with other amino acids. Meteoritic proline therefore demonstrates that proline or its precursors can survive at least part of asteroid/meteorite processing and reach planetary surfaces. It does not by itself identify whether that proline formed in interstellar ice, in aqueous parent-body chemistry, or through more than one process.\n\n**Assessment:** strong evidence for an extraterrestrial contribution to the proline inventory, but it addresses synthesis-and-delivery from astrophysical environments rather than an endogenous early-Earth route, and it offers no solution to L-specific homochirality.\n\n## Key intermediates, precursors, catalysts, and feedstock relationships\n\nProline is unusual among the standard proteinogenic amino acids because its side chain closes back onto the backbone nitrogen. Consequently, pathways that efficiently make glycine or alanine by an ordinary Strecker reaction do not automatically make proline. A viable proline network needs both an **\u03b1-amino-acid carboxyl/nitrile functionality and an intramolecular C\u2013N ring closure**. Patel et al.'s work makes this structural requirement particularly explicit.\n\nThe intermediates with the clearest experimental connection to proline are therefore:\n\n**Acrylonitrile \u2192 \u03b2-aminopropionitrile \u2192 3-aminopropanal \u2192 cyanohydrin \u2192 \u03b1-hydroxythioamide/cyclic thioamide \u2192 \u03b3-butyrothiolactam \u2192 pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile \u2192 proline.** This is the only surveyed prebiotic sequence in which the individual C\u2083 and cyclic intermediates on the proline branch are experimentally tracked in detail.\n\n**HCN** is a recurring central precursor even in routes that do not add it initially. It is deliberately supplied in the Hennet hydrothermal and Patel cyanosulfidic chemistry; it can arise among energetic products of formamide chemistry; and Hatanaka and Egami explicitly considered a common HCN intermediate plausible in their formaldehyde/hydroxylamine network. Atmospheric photochemical calculations also support possible HCN production under a broader range of atmospheric redox conditions than the classic CH\u2084-rich Miller atmosphere.\n\n**Formaldehyde** is another recurrent node. It is the direct carbon feedstock in the Fox\u2013Windsor and Hatanaka\u2013Egami experiments and one of the reagents in Hennet's hydrothermal experiments. In ordinary amino-acid chemistry it strongly favors C\u2081-derived products such as glycine, so proline necessarily signals additional carbon\u2013carbon condensation and ring-forming chemistry beyond a single Strecker event.\n\n**3-Aminopropanal (\u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde)** is probably the most important specifically identifiable C\u2083 proline precursor in the modern literature. Patel et al. generate it by sulfide-driven photoreduction of \u03b2-aminopropionitrile and then homologate/cyclize it into the proline branch. Because it already contains a terminal amino group and an aldehyde separated by three carbon atoms, it is structurally poised for construction of the eventual five-membered pyrrolidine framework.\n\nThe relation of the user's candidate primitive feedstocks to experimentally demonstrated routes is:\n\n| Primitive/low-complexity feedstock | Role in proline-relevant experiments |\n|---|---|\n| **H\u2082O** | Solvent/reactant in discharge, hydrothermal and surface chemistry; dominant interstellar ice constituent |\n| **CO\u2082** | Hydrothermal gas component; interstellar ice carbon source; plausible atmospheric reservoir, but not by itself a demonstrated direct proline precursor |\n| **CO** | Interstellar-ice starting material and potential C\u2081 precursor; not a stand-alone experimentally demonstrated terrestrial proline feedstock |\n| **NH\u2083 / NH\u2084\u207a** | Direct N source in discharge, Fox, hydrothermal, Patel and ice chemistry |\n| **N\u2082** | Direct atmospheric feedstock in Ring discharge experiments; must first be activated before incorporation |\n| **HCN / CN\u207b** | Central precursor in Patel and hydrothermal chemistry; generated or proposed in several other networks |\n| **H\u2082CO** | Direct feedstock in Fox, Hatanaka and Hennet routes |\n| **H\u2082S / HS\u207b** | Stoichiometric reductant and photochemical reagent in Patel's cyanosulfidic route |\n| **CH\u2084** | Carbon source in the Ring electric-discharge route |\n| **H\u2082** | Redox component in Hennet/Marshall-type hydrothermal chemistry and relevant to stabilization of organics at vents |\n| **HC\u2261CH** | Not in the user's candidate list but crucial in the Patel route because it supplies the C\u2082 fragment for acrylonitrile |\n| **NH\u2082OH** | Not a primitive feedstock in most models, but the direct N reagent in Hatanaka\u2013Egami chemistry |\n| **NH\u2082CHO** | Formamide is the direct C\u2081/N\u2081 feedstock in Saladino's irradiated-meteorite route |\n\nThese experimental facts show why it would be premature to select a single \u201cpreferred\u201d carbon source. **CH\u2084/N\u2082, HCHO/NH\u2083, HCN/acetylene, HCN/HCHO/NH\u2084\u207a, formamide, and CO/CO\u2082/methanol-containing ice all have experimental access to proline or a direct proline precursor**, but they solve different parts of the geochemical problem and have different environmental liabilities.\n\n### Catalysts and surfaces\n\nThe catalyst evidence is similarly heterogeneous. **No deliberately added mineral catalyst** is required in Fox\u2013Windsor thermal HCHO/NH\u2083 chemistry; electrical energy alone drives the Ring discharge experiment, although modern work demonstrates that the reactor's silicate surface can materially alter Miller chemistry.\n\nHatanaka and Egami provide direct evidence for a **molybdate-dependent change in selectivity**, but their active medium also contains Zn\u00b2\u207a, Fe\u00b3\u207a, Cu\u00b2\u207a, Co\u00b2\u207a, Mn\u00b2\u207a, kaolinite and montmorillonite. Consequently, the data establish environmental catalytic selection by a multicomponent inorganic medium, not a uniquely characterized single-site molybdate catalyst.\n\nHennet's hydrothermal experiments employ **pyrite\u2013pyrrhotite\u2013magnetite redox assemblages or illite**, making Fe\u2013S minerals and clay surfaces central to the simulated geochemical setting. In that framework the minerals are as important for controlling redox state and surface chemistry as for conventional heterogeneous catalysis.\n\nPatel's network uses **Cu(I)/Cu(II)** chemistry for nitrile-generation and photoredox processes and **HS\u207b** as the electron donor/reductant. Importantly, some sulfide-driven photoreductions can operate without Cu(I), so sulfide is not merely a co-catalyst: it is chemically consumed as reducing power.\n\nSaladino's route demonstrates a bona fide role for **meteoritic mineral assemblages** under particle irradiation, whereas the interstellar-ice route needs **no mineral surface at all**: UV photons acting on a frozen molecular matrix supply the radical chemistry.\n\n## Environmental context and geochemical plausibility\n\nThere is no single environment that is simultaneously the best supported in terms of **reaction mechanism, natural reagent supply, product yield, stereoselectivity and geological evidence**. Different routes dominate different criteria.\n\n### Atmosphere\u2013ocean interfaces and lightning\n\nElectric discharge has the virtue of beginning with extremely simple gases and water and of generating proline without human addition of an already complex C\u2083 or C\u2084 intermediate. Its liability is atmospheric composition. The Hadean zircon redox record argues strongly against a long-lived global CH\u2084/NH\u2083 atmosphere generated by ordinary mantle outgassing. Accordingly, the 1972 Ring experiment should be interpreted as proof of what **strongly reduced atmospheric microenvironments or episodes can do**, not as proof that this was the average Hadean atmosphere.\n\nTransient reducing conditions following large impacts, or localized volcanic environments, remain chemically distinguishable from global steady-state atmospheric composition. In addition, HCN chemistry may survive some weakening of the classic reducing-atmosphere assumption because photochemical modeling permits HCN in certain N\u2082-rich atmospheres without requiring methane to dominate.\n\n### Hot surface pools and volcanic/geothermal settings\n\nFox\u2013Windsor chemistry and Hatanaka\u2013Egami chemistry both fit, at least thermally, into **hot aqueous or evaporative environments** better than into a cold dilute ocean. Concentration is critical: 0.3 M formaldehyde and 0.05 M hydroxylamine in Hatanaka's reactors are concentration regimes more naturally compared with evaporative or repeatedly concentrated microenvironments than bulk seawater.\n\nThe problem is that concentration helps chemistry while worsening geochemical burden: one must explain not only production but **selective accumulation without rapid destruction or escape**. This is particularly severe for hydroxylamine and for 10 mM molybdate. Molybdenum's redox-sensitive geological record makes a globally molybdate-rich Hadean ocean implausible; highly localized Mo-bearing mineral environments are more defensible but have not been tied quantitatively to the Hatanaka concentrations.\n\n### Submarine hydrothermal systems\n\nHydrothermal environments are geologically attractive because H\u2082, reduced minerals, Fe\u2013S surfaces, thermal gradients and continuous fluid flow are inherent to water\u2013rock interaction. Hennet et al. show that a proline-containing amino-acid mixture can indeed form at 150 \u00b0C when HCN/HCHO/NH\u2084\u207a are present.\n\nThe main unresolved issue is **feedstock flux, not merely temperature**. Demonstrating synthesis from tenths-of-molar laboratory HCN/HCHO/NH\u2084\u207a does not establish that a natural vent supplied such concentrations. Hennet and colleagues themselves distinguished their experimental concentrations from actual vent-fluid compositions.\n\nTemperature creates a second tension. Marshall demonstrated rapid amino-acid-forming chemistry at roughly 200\u2013275 \u00b0C, while Bada, Miller and Zhao showed rapid irreversible amino-acid destruction around 240 \u00b0C. These results are not mutually contradictory: they imply a dynamically reacting system in which **formation, destruction, transport and quenching compete**. Yanagawa and Kobayashi further showed that high hydrogen fugacity can slow destruction, making cool vent margins and rapidly mixed hydrothermal fluids considerably more favorable than long residence in the hottest reaction zone.\n\nFor proline network design, the most defensible hydrothermal scenario is therefore not \u201cproline accumulates indefinitely at 250\u2013350 \u00b0C,\u201d but rather **precursors form or react in moderately hot reducing zones and products are transported toward cooler regions before destruction dominates**. That is an inference from the combined experimental literature, not a directly demonstrated natural flux model.\n\n### UV-irradiated surface environments\n\nPatel's cyanosulfidic chemistry is naturally associated with **surface or shallow environments exposed to ultraviolet light**, not deeply buried or deep-sea settings where the necessary UV flux is absent. It further requires spatial and temporal access to cyanide, reduced sulfur and copper chemistry.\n\nIts greatest environmental advantage is chemical integration: the same general HCN/sulfide photochemistry gives precursors to several biochemical subsystems rather than requiring a bespoke reaction solely for proline. Its greatest weakness is exactly the converse: a **large reaction network may require environmental sequencing** to prevent mutually incompatible reagents or products from reacting prematurely. The laboratory demonstrations establish chemical compatibility under controlled sequences, not the existence of a natural reactor that executes those sequences automatically.\n\n### Extraterrestrial synthesis and delivery\n\nThe interstellar-ice and meteoritic-formamide experiments remove the requirement that all synthesis occur on Earth. UV-irradiated ices demonstrably make amino-acid precursors, and actual meteorites contain proline. Together these observations make **exogenous supplementation of early Earth's proline inventory empirically credible**, though they do not quantify how large that flux was relative to endogenous synthesis.\n\nThe two extraterrestrial routes should not be conflated. Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. model **cold interstellar/pre-cometary ice photochemistry**, whereas Saladino et al. model **formamide plus meteoritic minerals under energetic-particle irradiation**. Their feedstocks, temperatures, energy sources and mechanisms are different despite both being \u201cspace chemistry.\u201d\n\nActual Murchison proline is important but mechanistically non-diagnostic. Parent-body aqueous Strecker-type reactions, inherited interstellar material, irradiation chemistry, or combinations of these can all contribute to meteoritic organics. Meteoritic detection therefore supports **availability and survivability**, not any unique formation mechanism.\n\n## The specific problem of L\u2011proline and biochemical congruence\n\nThe target specified here is **L-proline**, not merely proline. That distinction materially changes the assessment.\n\nNone of the principal direct synthesis experiments\u2014Ring electric discharge, Fox HCHO/NH\u2083, Hatanaka HCHO/NH\u2082OH, Hennet hydrothermal chemistry, Patel cyanosulfidic chemistry or Saladino formamide irradiation\u2014establishes a substantial **intrinsic L-selectivity from achiral starting materials**. Where stereochemistry has been carefully examined in analogous abiotic amino-acid systems, racemic products are the normal outcome.\n\nThe interstellar-ice experiments are particularly informative because enantiomer-resolved analysis was part of the design. Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. detected both enantiomeric forms of chiral amino acids rather than a biologically imposed L-only distribution, and Bernstein et al.'s independent experiment explicitly reported **racemic amino acids**. Thus astrophysical UV synthesis provides a source of the proline skeleton but not an established origin of L-proline homochirality.\n\nMeteoritic proline likewise does not solve the question. Engel and Nagy resolved proline among both D/L-containing amino-acid populations in Murchison. Meteoritic enantiomeric data are valuable for studying possible symmetry-breaking processes, but detection of proline in carbonaceous chondrites is not equivalent to demonstrating an abiotic route producing exclusively\u2014or even strongly preferentially\u2014L-proline.\n\nAccordingly, a realistic synthesis network should represent the problem as at least two modules:\n\n\\[\n\\text{achiral simple feedstocks}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{DL-proline / proline precursor}\n\\]\n\nfollowed by a separate, presently unresolved process:\n\n\\[\n\\text{DL-proline}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{enantioenrichment}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{predominantly L-proline}.\n\\]\n\nNo surveyed primary study closes both modules in a single geochemically demonstrated pathway.\n\nThere is also a biochemical-continuity issue. Modern biological proline formation is structurally associated with a glutamate-derived semialdehyde/pyrroline chemistry, whereas the best-resolved prebiotic route of Patel et al. uses acrylonitrile, aminopropionitrile, aminopropanal, thioamides and an aminonitrile. None of the direct prebiotic experiments surveyed establishes an enzyme-free, geochemically plausible analogue that cleanly reproduces the extant glutamate-to-proline sequence. The literature therefore currently favors **chemical accessibility over strict pathway continuity** for proline.\n\nThis lack of biochemical congruence should be treated as a discriminator between origin models rather than as an automatic rejection criterion. A continuity model would regard it as a substantial weakness; a \u201cfeedstock first, metabolism later\u201d model can accept environmental production of proline followed by later evolutionary replacement of the synthetic route. The experimental chemistry alone does not decide between those evolutionary interpretations.\n\n## Major open questions, route-by-route critiques, and bottlenecks\n\n### Ring/Miller-type electric discharge\n\nThe outstanding mechanistic question is **how the pyrrolidine ring forms**. Proline's small but reproducible presence demonstrates net synthesis, yet the route from discharge-generated C\u2081/C\u2082/C\u2083 compounds to a C\u2085 cyclic secondary amino acid has not been experimentally mapped in the way Patel's later pathway has.\n\nIts principal geochemical criticism is the strongly reducing CH\u2084/NH\u2083-rich atmosphere. Hadean zircon data argue against that as the normal product of mantle outgassing. A modern synthesis-network representation should therefore assign Ring chemistry to **localized/transient reducing atmospheric conditions unless an independent source of bulk CH\u2084/NH\u2083 is specified**.\n\nIts analytical/catalytic criticism is that vessel walls may matter. Criado-Reyes et al.'s glass-versus-Teflon experiments show that silicate surfaces can materially change electric-discharge chemistry. Old \u201cgas-phase\u201d experiments should therefore not automatically be modeled as homogeneous gas/water chemistry.\n\nFinally, the proline branch is low abundance and no L selectivity has been established. Those are serious limitations if the network requires proline as an abundant early monomer rather than a trace member of a large organic mixture.\n\n### Fox\u2013Windsor formaldehyde/ammonia route\n\nThe main bottleneck is mechanistic: **what hydrolysable precursor contains the proline framework?** The experiment establishes that proline is liberated after hydrolysis but does not identify the ring-forming intermediate.\n\nThe environmental bottlenecks are concentration and temperature. Heating HCHO/NH\u2083 to approximately 185 \u00b0C is chemically possible in transient hot environments, but a natural setting must simultaneously supply and retain substantial formaldehyde and ammonia. Neither the Science experiment nor subsequent observations show that those concentrations arise spontaneously in a realistic open Hadean pool.\n\nA further concern is that acid hydrolysis creates an observational boundary between \u201cmaterial capable of yielding proline\u201d and \u201cfree proline present before workup.\u201d Historical documentation of amino-acid contamination in strong-acid reagents adds a general caution for low-yield hydrolysis studies, although it does not by itself invalidate the Fox result.\n\n### Hatanaka\u2013Egami molybdate route\n\nThis experiment poses one of the sharpest **chemistry-versus-geochemistry tradeoffs** in the entire literature. Chemically, its proline selectivity is unusually strong: changing MoO\u2084\u00b2\u207b over four orders of magnitude changes proline from essentially undetectable to 2.75 mol% of the analyzed amino-acid residues. Geochemically, the most proline-favoring condition is 10\u207b\u00b2 M molybdate in a system already containing 0.3 M formaldehyde, 0.05 M hydroxylamine, five transition metals and abundant clays.\n\nBecause molybdenum was poorly mobilized under low-O\u2082 conditions even much later in Earth history, a 10 mM Hadean molybdate reservoir requires a **specific concentrating/mineralogical mechanism**, not merely an appeal to \u201cseawater.\u201d The hydroxylamine supply remains similarly unresolved.\n\nA mechanistic deconvolution experiment would also be valuable: HCHO/NH\u2082OH with Mo alone, each transition metal alone, specific clay surfaces alone, and factorial combinations could reveal whether proline selectivity arises from soluble molybdate speciation, clay adsorption, redox cycling, or cooperative catalysis. The 1977 experiments demonstrate the phenomenon but do not uniquely assign the active catalytic chemistry.\n\n### Hydrothermal route\n\nThe first unresolved problem is **natural precursor concentration**. Hennet et al. deliberately used strong HCN/HCHO/NH\u2084\u207a solutions to test chemical possibility and explicitly cautioned against reading those concentrations as measured natural vent fluids. A successful origin model must therefore supply an upstream process concentrating those molecules.\n\nThe second is the formation/destruction competition. Bada, Miller and Zhao's 240 \u00b0C experiments make clear that \u201cthermodynamically allowed\u201d does not mean \u201ckinetically persistent.\u201d Any quantitative model of proline supply from hot vents must include residence time and irreversible decomposition rather than equilibrium free energies alone.\n\nThe strongest response to that critique is that natural hydrothermal systems have large temperature gradients and reducing H\u2082-rich fluids. Yanagawa and Kobayashi show experimentally that H\u2082 fugacity slows amino-acid decomposition. Thus the relevant quantity for a synthesis network is not simply a single vent temperature, but an **integrated production\u2013destruction\u2013transport flux through a temperature/redox gradient**.\n\n### Cyanosulfidic photoredox route\n\nHere the chemistry is much better resolved, so the bottleneck shifts to environmental integration. A network must explain simultaneous or sequential access to **HCN, acetylene, NH\u2083, sulfide, copper and UV irradiation** at useful concentrations. Demonstrating each step separately does not yet demonstrate autonomous geochemical execution of the whole sequence.\n\nThere is also no measured natural flux through the full proline branch. High step yields in individually tested transformations cannot simply be multiplied into a realistic environmental yield because dilution, competing reactions, photodestruction, mineral binding and reagent timing can alter branching ratios.\n\nMost importantly for the wording \u201cprebiotic synthesis of proline,\u201d the demonstrated endpoint is an **aminonitrile precursor**. Quantitative hydrolysis of that precursor to proline under the same geochemical sequence remains a desirable direct experiment.\n\n### Irradiated formamide/meteorite route\n\nThe primary environmental issue is **formamide activity**. An astronomical detection of formamide or a laboratory synthesis from dilute precursors does not automatically justify a pool of neat NH\u2082CHO. Quantifying plausible concentration by freezing, evaporation, mineral adsorption or extraterrestrial processing is therefore essential before this route can be compared fairly with aqueous HCN chemistry.\n\nThe proline-specific mechanism is also unresolved. Saladino et al.'s generic Strecker/radical framework accounts for amino-acid production in broad terms, but proline's ring closure demands additional chemistry. Isotope-resolved mechanistic studies would be especially useful because they could identify which formamide-derived carbon atoms become the \u03b1-carbon, carboxyl carbon and three ring methylene carbons of proline.\n\n### Interstellar-ice route\n\nThe main mechanistic ambiguity is whether the cold irradiated ice contains **free proline, an aminonitrile, a macromolecular precursor, or several of these forms before laboratory hydrolysis**. This matters greatly when estimating delivery and subsequent aqueous availability on Earth.\n\nThe environmental challenge is downstream rather than upstream: once generated in space, the material must survive warming, parent-body alteration, atmospheric entry and subsequent terrestrial processing. Actual meteoritic proline shows that complete destruction is not inevitable, but does not quantify the fraction delivered intact.\n\nAnd, as with all other demonstrated routes, the ice chemistry does not explain why terrestrial proteins ultimately selected **L-proline** rather than a racemate.\n\n## Implications for prebiotic synthesis-network design\n\nFor network construction, it would be misleading to encode a single edge such as\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{HCN + NH_3 + carbonyl \\rightarrow Pro}\n\\]\n\nand treat proline as just another ordinary Strecker product. The literature instead supports at least three fundamentally different **ring-construction logics**. The first is unresolved emergent cyclization inside highly complex discharge, formaldehyde/polymer or hydrothermal reaction networks. The second is the explicit **3-aminopropanal \u2192 thioamide \u2192 \u03b3-butyrothiolactam \u2192 cyclic aminonitrile** pathway of Patel et al. The third comprises radical-rich solid-state or irradiation networks in formamide and astrophysical ices for which the detailed ring-forming sequence is not yet assigned.\n\nA useful evidence hierarchy for a synthesis database would therefore distinguish:\n\n| Evidence class | Proline examples |\n|---|---|\n| **Direct free/analytically released proline detected** | Ring discharge; Fox hydrolysate; Hatanaka hydrolysate; Hennet hydrothermal mixture; Saladino irradiated formamide; Mu\u00f1oz Caro ice residue |\n| **Immediate structurally defined precursor demonstrated** | Patel pyrrolidine \u03b1-aminonitrile |\n| **Mechanistic ring-forming intermediates identified** | Patel: 3-aminopropanal, cyclic \u03b1-hydroxythioamide, \u03b3-butyrothiolactam |\n| **Environmental occurrence supporting delivery** | Proline in Murchison |\n| **L-selective synthesis demonstrated** | **None of the surveyed routes** |\n\nThe most useful intermediate to prioritize experimentally is **3-aminopropanal**. It connects a small, readily conceptualized C\u2083 nitrogen-containing precursor to a chemically explicit proline pathway and could in principle be supplied by chemistry other than Patel's acrylonitrile photoreduction. Testing its formation from multiple primitive feedstock sets\u2014atmospheric discharge products, hydrothermal nitriles, formaldehyde/ammonia networks and mineral-mediated chemistry\u2014would establish whether today's apparently separate proline frameworks actually converge on a common intermediate.\n\nA second priority is to distinguish **free proline from \u201cproline released by analytical hydrolysis.\u201d** Fox\u2013Windsor, Hatanaka\u2013Egami and interstellar-ice experiments demonstrate valuable precursor chemistry, but a network intended to model monomer availability should not assign the entire hydrolyzed analytical yield to a contemporaneous environmental pool of free proline.\n\nA third is quantitative isotope tracing. ^13C/^15N feedstocks can determine whether every atom of recovered proline derives from the intended precursors, resolve carbon-skeleton assembly, and greatly reduce uncertainty from contamination in hydrolysis-based experiments. The interstellar-ice work illustrates the strength of labeled substrates for this purpose; similar experiments would be especially valuable for HCHO/NH\u2083, HCHO/NH\u2082OH and formamide routes.\n\nA fourth is environmental concentration modeling linked directly to reaction kinetics. The critical unanswered quantities are not merely whether HCN, HCHO, NH\u2082OH, MoO\u2084\u00b2\u207b, acetylene or formamide **can exist**, but whether a realistic setting can maintain the activities used experimentally for long enough to outrun hydrolysis, photodestruction, thermal decomposition and dilution. The hydrothermal literature is particularly clear that kinetic lifetime must accompany thermodynamic feasibility.\n\nFinally, **homochirality should be represented as a separate unresolved module** rather than silently assigning an abiotic \u201cproline\u201d yield to L-proline. There is good experimental support for prebiotic access to the proline molecular skeleton in several environments, but no comparably established route from those achiral feedstocks directly to a predominantly L-proline inventory.\n\nOn the present evidence, the most defensible overall conclusion is therefore **pluralistic rather than route-exclusive**. Ring et al. establish that highly reduced atmospheric discharge can make proline; Fox and Hatanaka\u2013Egami show that formaldehyde-based aqueous chemistry can reach it, with remarkable mineral-dependent selectivity in the latter case; Hennet establishes compatibility with a hydrothermal cyanide/carbonyl network; Patel et al. provide by far the clearest molecular pathway to the proline aminonitrile; Saladino et al. and Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. establish independent irradiation-driven extraterrestrial routes. None simultaneously satisfies high yield, minimal chemical complexity, fully demonstrated mechanism, strong Hadean geochemical support, direct free-proline production and L-selectivity. That absence of a universally dominant route is the central current state of the prebiotic L-proline problem.\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH ===\n\nConsider how well the network aligns with this literature in your evaluation.\n\n\nPlease evaluate the network and respond with JSON:\n{\n    \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n        {\n            \"pathway_id\": \"<id>\",\n            \"reaction_scores\": {\"rxn_001\": 0.85, ...},\n            \"chemical_feasibility\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"pathway_coherence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"environmental_progression\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"target_achievement\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"feedback\": \"<pathway-specific feedback>\",\n            \"approved\": <true if confidence >= 0.8>,\n            \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_ids with issues\"],\n            \"suggestions\": [\"improvements\"]\n        }\n    ],\n    \"best_pathway_id\": \"<id of best pathway>\",\n    \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n    \"network_feedback\": \"<overall assessment>\"\n}\n\nNote: \"environmental_progression\" scores Environmental Consistency (conditions fit each\nreaction's stated environment), not any particular order of environments.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.77,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.76\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.87,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.92,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.7,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.83,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.83,\n      \"feedback\": \"The identified intermediates form a coherent proline-skeleton route closely aligned with Patel et al. (2015). Aci