0 K; proposed operating screen, not a quoted experimental optimum", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar, with replenished gas-liquid contact", "catalyst": "Cu(I) stabilized by cyanide ligands", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, initially dark gas-contacting pool. Proposed screening concentrations: 10-50 mM total HCN/CN-, 1-20 mM dissolved acetylene, and 10-100 micromolar total copper; pH 7-8.5. Screen hours to one day. Introduce sulfide only downstream because copper sulfide precipitation can remove the catalyst. Retention of volatile HCN and acetylene is an unresolved field requirement."}, "mechanism": "Copper-mediated addition of HCN across the acetylene triple bond forms acrylonitrile. Reference balance: mol_002 + mol_003 -> mol_007.", "reasoning": "Supported transformation: Patel et al. (2015) connect HCN/acetylene chemistry to the acrylonitrile entry of the proline branch. Copper is assigned a coordination-catalytic role, not a generic mineral-surface role. The numerical operating screen above is proposed here rather than presented as the published protocol. A naturally available, soluble copper-cyanide pool and staged exclusion of sulfide remain environmental bottlenecks; detection of copper minerals alone would not establish catalytic availability."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Ammonia conjugate addition to acrylonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 9-10, 1-30 mM acrylonitrile and 0.1-1 M total ammonia/ammonium, with ammonia in excess; hours to one day. Keep dark and add sulfide only after this stage. Excess ammonia and limited acrylonitrile availability are intended to suppress repeated cyanoethylation, but their natural attainment is not established."}, "mechanism": "Aza-Michael addition of ammonia to the terminal carbon of acrylonitrile gives 3-aminopropanenitrile. Reference balance: mol_007 + mol_004 -> mol_008.", "reasoning": "Supported transformation: Patel et al. (2015) report approximately 83% conversion in their tested segment. That value is not assigned to the proposed concentration and pH window or to an environmental reactor. Overaddition and competing polymerization can divert material, and the ammonia-rich stage cannot simply be assumed compatible with all subsequent reactions."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Sulfide-driven photoreduction of beta-aminopropionitrile", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_005", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)/Cu(II) cyanide photoredox complexes"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Copper-cyanide photoredox pool; its substrate-specific contribution requires verification", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: 1-20 mM aminonitrile, 5-100 mM total dissolved sulfide, 10-100 micromolar copper, pH 7-8.5, and UV within the short-wavelength surface-accessible spectrum. A 254 nm laboratory source is a reference test, not a demonstrated solar dose. Sample over approximately 0.5-12 hours and capture the aminoaldehyde promptly. Measure dissolved copper and sulfide because copper sulfide precipitation can suppress catalysis."}, "mechanism": "Photochemical reducing equivalents supplied by sulfide reduce the nitrile to an imine-level intermediate; hydrolysis releases 3-aminopropanal and ammonia. Formal sulfur-endmember balance: 8 mol_008 + 8 mol_005 + 8 mol_001 -> 8 mol_009 + 8 mol_004 + mol_006. S8 represents an oxidized sulfur reservoir, not an established exclusive photoproduct.", "reasoning": "Supported substrate transformation: Patel et al. (2015) report approximately 45% conversion to beta-aminopropionaldehyde in an optimized segment. This network does not introduce a supposedly validated copper-free alternative for this particular substrate. Copper is included in the proposed implementation, but the supplied summary does not establish its separate quantitative benefit at the stated concentrations. Sulfide is a consumed reductant, not merely an agent. Sulfur speciation, aldehyde lifetime, UV dose and achievable environmental throughput remain unclosed."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Cyanohydrin capture of 3-aminopropanal", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 6.5-8, freshly generated aminoaldehyde at 1-20 mM and one to five molar equivalents of total cyanide; minutes to hours in a shaded receiving microenvironment. Transfer promptly from irradiation and avoid prolonged storage of the free aminoaldehyde. No quantitative purification from residual ammonia, copper or sulfide is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, followed by proton transfer, producing 4-amino-2-hydroxybutanenitrile. Reference balance: mol_009 + mol_002 -> mol_010. The new stereocenter is not assigned an enantiomeric preference.", "reasoning": "Supported transformation: Patel et al. (2015) report essentially quantitative cyanohydrin formation under their tested conditions. That observation supports rapid capture as a chemically meaningful operation, but does not demonstrate the proposed transfer or concentration window. Aminoaldehyde oligomerization and competing imine chemistry must be assessed in the actual preceding reaction mixture."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Sulfide addition to the aminoaldehyde cyanohydrin nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 7.5-9, 1-20 mM cyanohydrin and 5-100 mM total sulfide; monitor over hours to days. Use a shaded compartment for the addition stage before the later irradiation step. Sulfide is a stoichiometric sulfur reagent here."}, "mechanism": "Hydrosulfide addition to the nitrile followed by proton transfer and tautomerization generates the alpha-hydroxythioamide manifold. Reference balance: mol_010 + mol_005 -> mol_011.", "reasoning": "Supported reaction manifold: Patel et al. (2015) describe cyanohydrin-derived alpha-hydroxythioamides en route to cyclic thioamides. This node represents the structurally relevant connectivity, not proof that a single pure thioamide accumulates exclusively or is isolated at this stage. The proposed pH, concentrations and residence times require substrate-specific testing."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Five-membered thiolactam ring formation", "inputs": ["mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 7-9 and millimolar hydroxythioamide, monitored over 1-48 hours. Test lower-ammonia aliquots rather than assuming that the ammonia-rich mixture from rxn_002 cyclizes equally efficiently. Exchange, dilution or prior ammonia loss would need to occur without losing the organic substrate; no automatic environmental separation is assumed."}, "mechanism": "The terminal amino group attacks the thioamide carbon intramolecularly. Elimination of ammonia produces the five-membered alpha-hydroxythiolactam. Reference balance: mol_011 -> mol_012 + mol_004.", "reasoning": "Supported ring-construction logic: Patel et al. (2015) identify formation and cyclization within the hydroxythioamide manifold. Five-membered ring closure supplies the structural operation missing from an ordinary acyclic Strecker synthesis. The separate node is a mechanistic decomposition of that manifold, not a claim that every intermediate was independently isolated or that ammonia removal has been demonstrated in a natural pool."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Photoreductive deoxygenation to gamma-butyrothiolactam", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)/Cu(II) cyanide photoredox complexes"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Copper-cyanide photoredox pool, included as a proposed implementation rather than asserted to be universally necessary", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 7-8.5, 1-20 mM cyclic hydroxythioamide, 10-100 mM total sulfide and 10-100 micromolar copper. Apply UV in a thin water layer and follow substrate loss and thiolactam formation during an initial 0.5-12 hour dose screen. Limit continued exposure after product accumulation."}, "mechanism": "Sulfide-supported photoreduction removes the alpha-hydroxyl functionality while retaining the thiolactam ring. Formal sulfur-endmember balance: 8 mol_012 + 8 mol_005 -> 8 mol_013 + 8 mol_001 + mol_006.", "reasoning": "Supported transformation sequence: Patel et al. (2015) connect the cyclic hydroxythioamide manifold to gamma-butyrothiolactam by irradiation/reduction. The proposed copper-containing operating window is not a claim of a published catalyst-omission comparison for this substrate. Product selectivity and sulfur speciation must be measured; the S8 balance does not demonstrate that pure cyclooctasulfur is the actual sole oxidation product."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Thiolactam photoreduction followed by cyanide trapping", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_002", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)/Cu(II) cyanide photoredox complexes"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Copper-cyanide photoredox pool in the reduction stage", "aqueous": true, "other": "Two-stage operation: first irradiate millimolar thiolactam with a proposed 10-100 mM sulfide pool at pH 7-8.5; then limit irradiation and provide cyanide for trapping. Screen one to five cyanide equivalents during trapping rather than assuming arbitrary simultaneous mixing is equivalent. Copper solubility, UV dose, sulfide availability and the timing of cyanide exposure must be verified experimentally."}, "mechanism": "Photoreduction/desulfurization accesses a transient cyclic imine or its aqueous precursor, followed by cyanide capture to form pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile. The transient imine is not asserted to be an independently isolated compound. Formal sulfur-endmember balance: 8 mol_013 + 8 mol_002 + 8 mol_005 -> 8 mol_014 + 8 mol_005 + mol_006. Sulfide appears on both sides because donor oxidation and substrate desulfurization can consume and regenerate sulfide pools; this is not evidence of quantitative reductant recycling.", "reasoning": "Supported immediate-precursor endpoint: Patel et al. (2015) report approximately 32% conversion through the final two-step sequence to the proline aminonitrile. This is not a measured yield of free proline. The sequence is represented as a composite reaction to avoid promoting a transient imine to an experimentally isolated intermediate. Sulfur redistribution and the S8 endmember remain formal bookkeeping rather than a measured coproduct inventory. No stereochemical bias is established."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Proposed acidic surface hydrolysis of proline aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed, unvalidated screen: 0.1-10 mM aminonitrile, pH 0-1, and residence times from one day to several weeks in a shaded acidic geothermal pool. Test separate aliquots after photochemistry; directly acidifying the complete cyanide/sulfide broth is not assumed to be selective. A persistent strongly acidic reservoir, retention of the organic substrate and control of residual copper/sulfide are required. Quantify aminonitrile, intermediate prolinamide, free proline and decomposition products throughout."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed nitrile hydration followed by amide hydrolysis releases the carboxylic acid and ammonia/ammonium. Reference balance for an expected racemic precursor pool: 2 mol_014 + 4 mol_001 -> mol_015 + mol_016 + 2 mol_004. Equal L and D coefficients specify the achiral-model expectation, not an experimentally measured enantiomeric ratio.", "reasoning": "Proposed extension, not a demonstrated continuation of the cited synthesis: Patel et al. (2015) establish the aminonitrile endpoint but do not provide a complete measured free-proline synthesis under this regime. Nitrile-to-amide-to-acid conversion is conventional chemistry, yet rates, retro-Strecker loss, substrate survival and free-proline recovery must be established for this specific compound and mixture. Strong acidity is a localized environmental assumption, not a generally available prebiotic reagent. This route is not ranked above alkaline or hydrothermal completion without comparative data."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Proposed alkaline surface hydrolysis of proline aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous hydroxide ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Base catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed, unvalidated screen: 0.1-10 mM aminonitrile, pH 10.5-11.5, and days-to-months residence in a shaded, locally alkaline evaporative or geothermal pool. Measure pH during evaporation rather than treating it as constant. Separate this prolonged processing stage from UV synthesis. Complete conversion cannot be assumed at these relatively moderate hydroxide activities; accumulation of prolinamide or decomposition instead of proline is a possible outcome."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide-promoted nitrile hydration and subsequent amide hydrolysis produce prolinate and ammonia; the molecular node records the corresponding neutral reference composition. Reference balance: 2 mol_014 + 4 mol_001 -> mol_015 + mol_016 + 2 mol_004.", "reasoning": "Proposed extension: the supplied research contains no substrate-specific validation of alkaline conversion of the Patel et al. (2015) proline aminonitrile under these conditions. A plausible reaction class does not establish an adequate environmental rate. Mildly alkaline processing may avoid requiring a concentrated acid reservoir, but potentially slow amide cleavage, competing reactions, dilution and long residence times remain serious limitations. No alkaline advantage or L-selectivity is claimed."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Proposed confined geothermal hydrolysis and rapid quench", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "390-423 K during processing; quench below 330 K afterward", "pressure": "10-30 bar, sufficient to retain liquid water", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: 0.1-10 mM aminonitrile, approximately pH 6-8 at reaction temperature, and 0.5-24 hour residence times. Surface-derived precursor would need transport into shallow, confined geothermal fractures, not irradiation at depth. Determine precursor survival during transport. Quench the exiting fluid by rapid dilution or heat exchange, with a design target below 330 K within one minute. These residence and quench targets are proposed tests, not published proline production measurements."}, "mechanism": "Thermally accelerated hydration and amide cleavage convert the nitrile into a carboxyl group. Reference balance: 2 mol_014 + 4 mol_001 -> mol_015 + mol_016 + 2 mol_004. Cooling is a preservation operation, not a separate molecule-forming reaction.", "reasoning": "Proposed extension: Patel et al. (2015) do not demonstrate this hydrothermal completion. Bada et al. (1995) show why formation must be evaluated against irreversible thermal destruction, while Yanagawa and Kobayashi (1997) demonstrate that reducing conditions can affect amino-acid stability. Those studies do not supply rate constants for this particular aminonitrile. Moderate temperature and quenching are defensible design choices, but neither proves that hydrolysis outruns precursor and product destruction. No unspecified mineral catalyst is added."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Reducing atmospheric discharge generation and aqueous capture of HCN", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_018"], "outputs": ["mol_002", "mol_019"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K in the receiving water; electrical discharge is a nonthermal energy source", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Transient CH4/N2-rich reducing gas with water vapor and possible trace ammonia, coupled to a cooler aqueous receiver. Proposed receiver pH: 8.5-9.5 to aid cyanide retention. Capture and subsequently concentrate the aqueous cyanide fraction before supplying rxn_001, rxn_004 and rxn_008. No natural discharge duration, capture efficiency or millimolar cyanide inventory is established here. Sulfide-rich copper photochemistry is downstream, not assumed to occur inside the discharge apparatus."}, "mechanism": "Electrical activation of carbon and nitrogen species produces HCN among a complex gas-phase network; water captures part of that inventory. Formal target-branch balance: 2 mol_017 + mol_018 -> 2 mol_002 + 3 mol_019. This equation is not a measured discharge product balance.", "reasoning": "Supported source class, with an inferred network coupling: Ring et al. (1972) establish productive reducing-gas discharge chemistry, and Rugheimer and Rimmer (2019) support atmospheric HCN availability more broadly through modeling. The particular coupling to the Patel et al. (2015) sequence is proposed, not experimentally integrated. Trail et al. (2011) argue against treating a globally persistent highly reducing atmosphere as the default, so this source is limited to localized or transient conditions. Its inclusion supplies an actual required reagent; it does not establish acetylene, ammonia, copper or sulfide co-availability."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Empirical reducing-gas discharge synthesis and recovery of proline", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_004", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_019"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Silicate reactor or shoreline interface, with catalytic contribution unresolved"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370 K bulk water with a cooler condenser or receiving zone", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "No deliberately added catalyst is required by the reported route; silicate surfaces may influence the product distribution", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported feedstock family: CH4, N2, water and trace NH3 under electrical discharge. The supplied report does not specify an exact reproducible gas ratio, discharge dose or residence time, so none is presented as measured. A proposed comparison uses a days-long gas-water circulation experiment with matched silicate and inert-vessel controls. Separate unhydrolyzed and hydrolyzed analytical aliquots and use isotope-labeled feeds and blanks. Any strong-acid workup belongs to analytical recovery, not an assumed natural pond process."}, "mechanism": "Empirical composite module: discharge-generated carbon/nitrogen intermediates undergo unresolved carbon-chain construction and pyrrolidine-ring formation, followed by analytical recovery. This is not encoded as an ordinary one-step Strecker mechanism. One balanced reference allocation is: 20 mol_017 + mol_018 + 2 mol_004 + 8 mol_001 -> 2 mol_015 + 2 mol_016 + 33 mol_019. The allocation does not identify actual nitrogen provenance, conversion or coproduct distribution.", "reasoning": "Direct endpoint evidence: Ring et al. (1972) detected proline, at approximately 1.5 micromoles, in a much larger amino-acid mixture. No substantial L-selectivity or resolved proline-specific mechanism was demonstrated. Criado-Reyes et al. (2021) show that silicate vessel composition can materially affect discharge chemistry, justifying an explicit surface-control requirement without assigning a proven proline-specific silicate catalyst. Trail et al. (2011) restrict the environmental interpretation to plausible transient or local reducing episodes. This supports laboratory recovery more strongly than a quantified natural proline flux."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Empirical thermal formaldehyde-ammonia synthesis followed by hydrolytic recovery", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_022", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 458 K for the reported thermal synthesis; analytical hydrolysis is a separate subsequent operation", "pressure": "Confined aqueous synthesis; approximately 20-50 bar is a proposed retention envelope, not the reported reactor pressure", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported observation: heating aqueous formaldehyde/ammonia near 185 C produces material that releases proline upon hydrolysis. The supplied report does not give exact feed concentrations or heating duration. A proposed implementation screen is 0.1-1 M formaldehyde and total ammonia, sampled over 1-24 hours, without claiming reproduction of the original yield. A separate proposed analytical reference is sealed 6 M HCl hydrolysis near 383 K for about 24 hours, with isotope tracing and reagent blanks; this is not asserted to be the original protocol or a naturally available workup."}, "mechanism": "Empirical synthesis-and-workup module involving unresolved C-C construction, nitrogen incorporation, ring formation and hydrolysis of product material. No specific polymer or aminonitrile is invented. Formal reference balance: 12 mol_020 + 2 mol_004 -> mol_015 + mol_016 + 2 mol_022 + 4 mol_001. Formic acid is a proposed oxidized-carbon balancing sink, not an established stoichiometric coproduct of the proline-producing branch.", "reasoning": "Hydrolysate endpoint evidence: Fox and Windsor (1970) reported proline after hydrolysis of heated formaldehyde/ammonia products. Wolman et al. (1971) and the historical hydrolysis-blank concerns summarized in the supplied research motivate stringent controls rather than dismissal of the result. No mineral catalyst is added because none is required by the reported recipe. The 458 K step requires confinement and cannot be assigned to an ordinary surface pool. The natural concentration mechanism, unresolved proline-bearing precursor and difference between analytical release and environmental free-proline availability remain major gaps."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Empirical formaldehyde-hydroxylamine multimetal-clay synthesis and hydrolytic recovery", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_021"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_022", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Molybdate at 0.1 mM as a selectivity-modifying component", "Kaolinite at approximately 2% w/v", "Montmorillonite at approximately 2% w/v", "Zn2+, Fe3+, Cu2+, Co2+ and Mn2+, each at approximately 0.1 mM in the laboratory reference medium"], "conditions": {"temperature": "373-383 K, corresponding to the reported 105 plus or minus 5 C synthesis", "pressure": "Sealed anoxic vessel; 2-5 bar is a proposed retention envelope, not a reported measured pressure", "catalyst": "Multicomponent metal-clay medium; no unique molybdate-only catalytic mechanism is established", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported reference conditions: 0.30 M formaldehyde, 0.05 M hydroxylamine, initial pH 5.5, nitrogen atmosphere and seven days of heating. Select the reported 0.1 mM molybdate condition, not the 10 mM maximum-selectivity condition. Strong-acid analytical hydrolysis follows synthesis; a separate proposed reference workup is 6 M HCl near 383 K for about 24 hours, not asserted to be the original detailed protocol. This is a confined geothermal analogue because the temperature exceeds the specified ambient-pressure surface range. Trace Fe3+ is retained as a laboratory-medium component; no molecular oxygen or globally ferric environment is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Empirical composite module: hydroxylamine/formaldehyde chemistry enters unresolved oxime, condensation and nitrogenous-product networks, with subsequent C5-ring formation and hydrolytic release. HCN involvement is possible but is not encoded as established. Formal reference balance: 14 mol_020 + 2 mol_021 -> mol_015 + mol_016 + 4 mol_022 + 4 mol_001. Formic acid is an unverified redox-balancing allocation rather than a measured proline-branch coproduct.", "reasoning": "Hydrolysate endpoint and selectivity evidence: Hatanaka and Egami (1977) report approximately 0.16 mol% proline among analyzed amino-acid residues at 0.1 mM molybdate. The 2.75 mol% result belongs to 10 mM molybdate and is not transferred to this lower-Mo network. Scott et al. (2008) support strong caution about dissolved Mo availability in poorly oxygenated settings; even 0.1 mM requires a local enrichment explanation. Hydroxylamine supply and concentration are external assumptions. The exact ferric-containing multimetal recipe is a laboratory comparator: replacing Fe3+ with abundant environmental Fe2+ cannot be assumed to preserve the result. This route remains conditional rather than a demonstrated executable Hadean process."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Empirical reducing hydrothermal cyanide-formaldehyde-ammonium synthesis and recovery", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_020", "mol_004", "mol_019"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite assemblage as a redox-buffering mineral system"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 423 K synthesis, followed by quenching below 330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 10 bar total", "catalyst": "Mineral redox-buffering assemblage; a proline-specific heterogeneous catalytic site is not established", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported framework: concentrated cyanide/formaldehyde/ammonium solutions near 150 C and 10 bar with reducing mineral conditions. Because exact concentrations and residence times are not supplied in the report, a proposed test uses 0.1 M each of total cyanide, formaldehyde and ammonium, initial room-temperature pH 8-9, 1-5 bar H2 partial pressure within the total pressure budget, and 0.5-24 hour residence screens. These are design choices, not claimed original values or natural vent concentrations. H2 may be supplied locally by water-rock chemistry; the graph does not require transport of atmospheric H2 to a vent. Quench within a proposed one-minute target and analyze unhydrolyzed and hydrolyzed fractions separately."}, "mechanism": "Empirical hydrothermal reaction network produces a proline-containing amino-acid mixture through unresolved chain assembly and ring closure. Ordinary Strecker chemistry alone is not asserted to explain the pyrrolidine ring. One formal reducing-branch balance is: mol_002 + 9 mol_020 + mol_004 + 3 mol_019 -> mol_015 + mol_016 + 5 mol_001. This is not a measured hydrogen consumption or product mass balance.", "reasoning": "Direct analytical endpoint evidence: Hennet et al. (1992) report proline under the stated hydrothermal framework and explicitly caution that their concentrated precursors were not claimed to represent natural fluids. Bada et al. (1995) require attention to thermal destruction, while Yanagawa and Kobayashi (1997) support considering hydrogen fugacity in preservation. Neither establishes net proline accumulation under the proposed residence-time screen. The mineral assemblage is justified as a redox/environmental control, not automatically as an isolated proline catalyst. Natural feedstock concentration, exact recovery conditions and synthesis-versus-destruction kinetics remain unresolved."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Supplied HCN to cyanosulfidic aminonitrile with acidic completion", "description": "Starts from supplied HCN, acetylene, ammonia, sulfide and water. Follows the experimentally supported ring-construction sequence and then the unvalidated acidic hydrolysis extension. L-proline is an expected component of the resulting DL mixture. This is a scaffold-supported pathway, not an experimentally completed environmental synthesis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Supplied HCN to cyanosulfidic aminonitrile with alkaline completion", "description": "Shares the demonstrated scaffold chemistry of P1 but evaluates alkaline nitrile and amide hydrolysis in a separate, shaded surface compartment. Its distinction is the chemical product-release regime, not an independent ring-forming mechanism. Useful rates and survival under prolonged alkaline exposure remain unmeasured.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_013", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Supplied HCN to surface scaffold formation and confined geothermal completion", "description": "Builds the aminonitrile at the UV-exposed surface, then proposes transport into shallow confined geothermal water for hydrolysis and rapid quenching. Precursor transport, hydrolysis rates and thermal survival must all be demonstrated. This route is not claimed to outperform either surface hydrolysis option.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_013", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Discharge-supplied HCN feeding cyanosulfidic synthesis and acidic completion", "description": "Extends P1 upstream by generating and capturing HCN from methane/nitrogen discharge chemistry. Captured HCN supplies the multiple cyanide-requiring stages; acetylene, ammonia and sulfide remain separate feedstocks. This is an explicitly different feedstock-supply sequence, not a second experimental validation of the downstream scaffold or hydrolysis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_012", "rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_002", "mol_009", "mol_013", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Discharge-supplied HCN feeding cyanosulfidic synthesis and alkaline completion", "description": "Combines explicit atmospheric cyanide supply with the scaffold sequence and the alkaline product-release test. Capture and concentration of atmospheric HCN are additional bottlenecks beyond those of P2. Neither the linked source-to-scaffold operation nor final free-proline yield has been demonstrated as one protocol.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_012", "rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_002", "mol_009", "mol_013", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Discharge-supplied HCN feeding surface scaffold formation and geothermal completion", "description": "Includes atmospheric HCN formation, surface aminonitrile assembly and proposed confined geothermal hydrolysis. It is a longer supply-and-processing variant of the same scaffold chemistry, not an independently demonstrated sixth proline synthesis. Both atmospheric capture and subsequent precursor transport require explicit flux and survival measurements.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_012", "rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_002", "mol_009", "mol_013", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Direct empirical reducing-atmosphere discharge family", "description": "Uses the Ring-type methane/nitrogen/water/trace-ammonia experiment as an empirical proline-producing module. Unlike P4-P6, it does not assume that the detected proline passed through the defined cyanosulfidic intermediates. The low-abundance endpoint, unresolved ring mechanism, vessel effects and transient reducing-atmosphere requirement are retained.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Thermal formaldehyde-ammonia synthesis with hydrolysate recovery", "description": "Represents formation of proline-releasing material from heated formaldehyde and ammonia, followed by analytical hydrolysis. The composite edge avoids inventing the uncharacterized precursor. Demonstrated laboratory recovery is distinguished from free-proline accumulation in a natural confined geothermal setting.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_014"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Formaldehyde-hydroxylamine synthesis in the lower-molybdate multicomponent medium", "description": "Retains the experimentally observed hydrolysate route at 0.1 mM molybdate, without borrowing the 10 mM molybdate selectivity result. Hydroxylamine supply, local Mo enrichment, metal oxidation states, clay effects and strong-acid recovery remain unresolved environmental requirements. It is a conditional laboratory-supported family, not a validated natural reactor.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_015"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Reducing hydrothermal cyanide-formaldehyde-ammonium family", "description": "Represents the Hennet-type proline-producing hydrothermal network with explicit reducing mineral control and a proposed residence-time/quen